hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"13311","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Shabi, who said: He was asked about people testifying that so-and-so is not the son of so-and-so. He said: When he establishes his lineage, even if like Rabia and Mudar came testifying, they would not remove him from his lineage."
"13312","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Whoever slanders a boy or a girl, there is no hadd upon him."
"13313","From al-Hasan ibn Umara, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, who said: There is no hadd upon the slanderer of a boy or girl."
"13314","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: Indirect speech? He said: There is no hadd in it. He, he and Umar, said: In it is exemplary punishment. He said: I said to him: Is he made to swear he did not intend such and such?"
"13315","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: A man said to his brother that his son is not his brother. He said: He is not given the hadd."
"13316","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that Umar used to apply the hadd for indirect accusation of immorality."
"13317","From Mamar, from Ismail ibn Umayya, who said: A man slandered a man in satire, or exposed him in it. He sought justice from him before Umar ibn al-Khattab, so he said to him: I did not mean this; I meant something else. The man said: Then name for you whom you meant. Umar said: You spoke truth; you have admitted vileness against yourself, or he said: the vile matter. So pin it upon whom you will. He did not mention anyone, so he flogged him the hadd. Ibn Jurayj reported: Ibn Abi Mulayka informed me, from Safwan and Ayyub, that he was given the hadd for indirect speech; and the one who applied the hadd for indirect speech was Umar ibn al-Khattab: Ikrima ibn Amir ibn Hashim ibn Abd Manaf ibn Abd al-Dar satirized Wahb ibn Zamaa ibn al-Aswad ibn Abd al-Muttalib ibn Asad, and alluded to him in his satire. Ibn Jurayj said: And I heard Ibn Abi Mulayka reporting that."
"13318","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who said to another: O son of the slave, or O slave. He said: He merely meant Abd Allah. He is made to swear by Allah he meant only that, and there is no hadd upon him; and if he refuses that, he is flogged."
"13319","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who said to another: O son of the weaver, O son of the tailor, O son of the cobbler, taunting him with some trades. He said: He is made to swear by Allah he did not intend denying him, and meant only his fathers trade; if he swears he is left, and if he refuses he is given the hadd."
"13320","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, who said: In indirect speech there is punishment."
"13321","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Amir ibn Masud, from Ibn al-Musayyab, that a man said to a man: O son of Abi Kirana. He said: He is struck the hadd, unless he produces evidence that it is a nickname."
"13322","From al-Thawri, from Ismail, from al-Shabi, he was asked about a man who said to a man: You are the pretender. He said: There is no hadd upon him; and even if he said: Six claimed you, there would be no hadd upon him."
"13323","From Sufyan, concerning a man who said to a man: O son of the Zinj woman. He said: He is struck when he transfers a lineage to a lineage."
"13324","From Mamar, from Qatada, who said: If a man said to another: I see you a fornicator, he is given tazir and not given the hadd; and all indirect speech is given tazir in, in Qatadas view."
"13325","From Mamar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ibn al-Musayyab, who said: The hadd is only upon one who sets the hadd plainly."
"13326","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, that he was asked about a man who said to a man: O son of the butcher. He said: It is nothing; we only know the hadd in plain qadhf and plain denial."
"13327","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Masud, who said: There is no hadd except in two: a man who denies from his father, or slanders a chaste woman. From Mamar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah, from al-Qasim, from Ibn Masud, the like of it."
"13328","From Mamar, from Qatada, who said: Ziyad said: Whoever alludes, we allude to him; and whoever declares, we declare to him. He said: And Qatada said: Tazir is given for indirect speech."
"13329","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I was told that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: Whoever alludes, we allude to him with whips; and he used to flog for indirect speech."
"13330","Ibn Jurayj reported: I heard Hafs ibn Umar ibn Rafi saying: There was a verbal dispute between my father and a Jew over preemption, so my father said to the Jew: A Jew, son of a Jew. He said: Yes, by Allah I am a Jew, son of a Jew, since many men do not know their fathers. The land agent wrote to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, while he was governor over Medina, about that, so he wrote: If the one who said that to him has a known father, then apply the hadd to the Jew. So he struck him eighty lashes."
"13331","Ibn Jurayj reported: I heard Muhammad ibn Hisham saying: A man in the rule of Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said to a man: You visit your slave women in secret. He said: By Allah I meant only date palms he used to steal. So Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz applied the hadd to him."
"13332","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: A man said to a man: O son of the collared one. So Hisham wrote about it to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, who wrote: If his father was not collared, apply the hadd to him."
"13333","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab was asked about a man to whom it was said: O son of the slave woman, though his father was not a slave woman. He said: He forbade flogging the hadd."
"13334","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab said, concerning a man who said to a man: O freedman, O pretender. He said: He is flogged the hadd."
"13335","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who said to another: Your mother was only picked up as a foundling. He said: He is flogged the hadd of slander, because he denied a woman from her father."
"13336","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Yahya ibn Said informed me that a man in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab said to a man: My mother is no fornicatress, nor my father a fornicator. Umar said: What do you think? They said: A man praised himself. He said: Rather it is, look: if the other has a fault, he has praised himself; and if he has no fault, why did he say it? By Allah I shall apply the hadd to him. So he applied the hadd to him."
"13337","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah, from Makhul, that Muadh ibn Jabal and Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As said: The hadd is only in the word that has no outlet, and has only one meaning."
"13338","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from a companion of his, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, from Ali, who said: When perhaps and maybe enter the hudud, the hadd is suspended."
"13339","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ata said: There is no hadd in saying: O drunkard, nor O thief, but flogging."
"13340","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: He said to another, until he says: You do with so-and-so."
"13341","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, concerning a man who says to a man: O Luti! He said: His intention is questioned, what did he intend by that."
"13342","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who says to another: You were flogged for adultery. He said: He is flogged eighty as the hadd of slander. He said: If he says: You were flogged a hadd for wine, he is given an exemplary punishment."
"13343","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man saying to another: O son of a Berber woman, O son of an Abyssinian woman, while his mother is an Arab. He said: There is no flogging upon him. He said: If he says: O son of so-and-so, for other than his father to whom he is attributed, he is struck the hadd."
"13344","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, concerning a man who says to a man: O Luti! They said: He is not subjected to hadd."
"13345","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who says to another: Your mother is not so-and-so. He said: He is not subjected to hadd until he denies him from his mother; this is a lie."
"13346","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, concerning a man who says to a man: You are not the son of so-and-so woman. He said: It is nothing."
"13347","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who says: He is an adulterer if he does not do such-and-such, then he does not do it. He said: I see he should be struck the hadd."
"13348","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il, from al-Sha'bi, that he was asked about a man who said to an Arab man: O Nabataean! He said: We are all Nabataeans; there is no hadd in this."
"13349","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata said: There is no hadd for saying: O drunkard, nor O thief, but there is flogging."
"13350","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Musa informed me, from Raja ibn Haywah, he said: Sulayman stood with us during his caliphate, and with him was Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and he said: What do you say about a man who says to a man: O drinker of wine? We said: He is subjected to hadd. Umar said: Glory be to Allah, the hadd is only upon one who slanders a Muslim."
"13351","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: When he says: O thief, O hypocrite, O disbeliever, O drinker of wine, he said: In all of this there is ta'zir."
"13352","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: When a man says to another: So-and-so claims you are an adulterer. He said: So-and-so is asked about that; if he acknowledges, otherwise the one who conveyed it is given ta'zir."
"13353","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: A man said to a man: So-and-so says you are an adulterer. He said: If he brings proof that he said that, there is nothing upon him except that evil is what he carried; and if he does not bring proof of that, the conveyor is flogged. And a man from the people of Kufa, while we were with Ata, said: The people of Kufa hold that when four testify against a man for adultery, and one of them steps forward to the imam, they say: He is in the position of a disputant, and they do not make him a witness; and if they come all at once, their testimony is accepted. Ata agreed with them on that. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: it is my concern and al-Mughirah's."
"13354","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached me from Amr ibn al-As while he was governor of Egypt, he said to a man from Tujib called Qanbarah: O hypocrite. He said: He went to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and Umar wrote to Amr: If he establishes proof against you, I will flog you ninety. So he entreated the people, and Amr confessed when they testified against him; they claimed Umar said to Amr: Declare yourself a liar upon the pulpit, and he did, and Amr offered himself to Qanbarah, and he forgave him for Allah, Mighty and Majestic."
"13355","From Ibrahim, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Abu Sufyan: Whoever says to a man: O effeminate, strike him twenty."
"13356","Ibrahim informed us, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Abu Sufyan, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever says to a man of the Ansar: O Jew, strike him twenty."
"13357","From Abdullah ibn Kathir, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Nadrah, from Sinan ibn Salamah ibn Muhabbiq, and Salamah had come to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: A man said to a man: O Luti! That was raised to Sinan ibn Salamah, and he said: What a good man you are if you are from the people of Lut."
"13358","Sufyan said, concerning a man who says to a man: You committed adultery in polytheism. He said: He is struck the hadd unless he brings proof, because he only slandered him now; and if he says: You committed adultery while you were a slave, he is struck the hadd; and if he says: You committed adultery while you were a child, he is not struck, because a child does not commit adultery."
"13359","From Sufyan, concerning a man who says to a woman who was a slave then freed: You committed adultery while you were a slave. He said: Proof is asked for that, otherwise he is struck the hadd, because he only slandered her while she is free."
"13360","Sufyan said, concerning one who says: I committed adultery with so-and-so woman. He said: She is asked; if she denies, he is struck the hadd for slandering her. Then it is said to him: If you testify against yourself four testimonies, we will establish the hadd upon you, and if you do not testify, we will not establish the hadd upon you."
"13361","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: A man said to a woman who was a slave then freed: You committed adultery, while you were for sale, and he did not bring proof of that. He said: He is flogged when he says that and does not bring proof for it. It was said to him: She had committed adultery while she was a slave. He said: Then there is no hadd."
"13362","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: A man said to a man four times: You committed adultery with so-and-so, naming her. He said: He is flogged one hundred if he is unmarried, and exiled a year, and stoned if he is previously married. I said: Is he not subjected to hadd for what he said? He said: One hadd suffices him. I said: They say: She is not subjected to hadd, and it must be that if she believes him about himself she believes him about her. He said: Rather I believe him about himself, and I do not believe him about her."
"13363","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, concerning a woman who slanders a man regarding herself, that he overpowered her, and the man denies that, and she has no proof. He said: She is struck the hadd of slander. Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri said so also."
"13364","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, concerning a man who says to his wife: I committed adultery with you before I married you. He said: He is flogged the hadd."
"13365","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who says: I committed adultery with so-and-so. He said: If he persists upon his statement, the hadd of slander and the hadd of adultery are established upon him."
"13366","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, he said: Upon one who spreads indecency there is exemplary punishment, even if he spoke truth."
"13367","Sufyan said: Concerning a man flogged the hadd, then a man says to him: O adulterer. He said: Averting with an excuse is preferred, and among us are those who say: When the hadd has been established upon him, whoever slanders him is flogged; and Ibn Abi Layla is among those who would not flog him."
"13368","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Ibn al-Musayyib was asked about a man who incurs a hadd, then a man reproaches him for it afterwards. He said: If repentance has been seen from him, the one who reproached him is given ta'zir."
"13369","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who says to a man: O adulterer, and to a woman: O adulteress, though they had been subjected to hadd before that. They said: He is given exemplary punishment for harming them, for the sanctity of the Muslim. He mentioned it from Ibn al-Musayyib."
"13370","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: There is no postponement in hudud except as much as the judge establishes."
"13371","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mis'ar, from Abu Awn, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Any man who testifies to a hadd while not present at it, that is only from malice."
"13372","From al-Thawri, from Jabbar and Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Testimony of a man upon testimony is not permitted in a hadd, nor is surety taken in a hadd."
"13373","From Isra'il, from Jabir, and from Amir, he said: Shurayh and Masruq would not permit testimony upon testimony in a hadd, nor would they take surety for one subject to hadd."
"13374","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, he said: When he slanders them all together, it is one hadd."
"13375","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about a man who slandered a group. He said: One hadd."
"13376","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me: he asked Tawus, he said: I said to him: A man entered upon a household and slandered them. He said: One hadd."
"13377","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: When a man slanders a group, it is one hadd."
"13378","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: If he slanders them all together, it is one hadd, whether they come together or separately."
"13379","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: He said in one utterance: O so-and-so, you are a prostitute. He said: One hadd. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: Two hadds. I said to Ata: Then he swore about various matters in one utterance and broke it? He said: Two expiations."
"13380","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: When he slanders a group, naming each person by his name, he is subjected to hadd for each person among them."
"13381","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, he said: I said to Tawus: He met people individually and slandered them? He said: One hadd."
"13382","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: He slandered a person, then went out and met another person and slandered him. He said: Two hadds."
"13383","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from the companions of Ibn Mas'ud, that they say: If a man slanders a man, then waits, then slanders another, it is only one hadd so long as he has not been subjected to hadd."
"13384","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani and Jabir and Firas, all from al-Sha'bi, concerning a man who slanders people all together. He said: If he separates, he is struck for each person among them, and if he combines, it is one hadd. From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim, like the statement of al-Sha'bi; from al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, like the statement of al-Sha'bi."
"13385","Al-Thawri said: And Ibn Abi Layla struck a woman with hudud in separate sittings, three hudud or four."
"13386","Ma'mar informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: When they come all together, it is one hadd, and if they come separately, hadd is applied for each person among them separately. Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, the like, and he added: And Urwah said: And the thief is likewise."
"13387","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that Umar ibn al-Khattab flogged a man the hadd, regarding the mother of a man who had died in Jahiliyyah, whom he slandered."
"13388","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that a man said to a man: O son of the banner-woman, and his mother had died in Jahiliyyah, so Marwan said to him: You shall bring proof she was a banner-woman, otherwise I will flog you; he did not bring proof, so he flogged him, because it used to be said to the prostitute: banner-woman."
"13389","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When she has a Muslim child, her slanderer is flogged for the sanctity of the Muslim."
"13390","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abu Bakr and those after him among the caliphs would flog one who calls a man's mother an adulteress, even if she were Jewish or Christian, for the sanctity of the Muslim, until Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz was appointed over Medina, and he had not heard anything about that, so he consulted about that, and Abdullah ibn Ubaydullah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab said to him: We do not see flogging a Muslim for a disbeliever, so he left the hadd after that day."
"13391","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Yahya ibn al-Mughirah informed me, that Makhramah ibn Nawfal slandered the mother of a man in Jahiliyyah, saying: I did with your mother in Jahiliyyah, and it reached Umar ibn al-Khattab and he said: No one shall repeat it after that."
"13392","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, concerning a man who slanders a Christian woman under a Muslim. He said: He is given exemplary punishment and not hadd. And he said: If he slanders a polytheist, there is punishment but no hadd."
"13393","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, that a man reproached a man for an indecency his mother had done in Jahiliyyah, and that was raised to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he said: There is no hadd upon him."
"13394","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, he said: If a slave slanders a free man, he is flogged forty, whether fortified by marriage to a free woman or not. I said: They say: He is flogged eighty. He denied that and recited: And those who accuse chaste women then do not bring four witnesses, flog them eighty lashes and never accept their testimony, and there is no testimony for a slave."
"13395","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Sulayman ibn Musa, from a man who went to Abd al-Malik asking him about things he named to me, and Abd al-Malik presented to Qabisah the writing in it: The slave slanders the free man. Qabisah said: He is flogged eighty."
"13396","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Ali narrating from his father that he informed him, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, that he struck a slave who slandered a free man forty. From al-Thawri, from Ja'far, from his father, from Ali, the like."
"13397","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Umar ibn Ata informed me, from Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, that he used to say: The hadd of a slave who slanders a free man is forty."
"13398","From Ma'mar, from a man, from al-Hasan, he said: If a slave slanders a free man, he is flogged forty."
"13399","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: He is flogged forty. Ma'mar said: And I did not see most of them except saying that."
"13400","From al-Thawri, from Dhakwan, from Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Rabi'ah, he said: I reached Umar and Uthman and those after them among the caliphs: they would not strike the owned slave in slander except forty."
"13401","From Malik, from Abu al-Zinad, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz flogged a slave in slander eighty. Abu al-Zinad said: So I asked Abdullah ibn Amir about that? He said: I reached Umar and the caliphs one after another, and I did not see anyone strike in slander more than forty."
"13402","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a slave who slanders a free man. He said: He is flogged eighty."
"13403","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: A man slandered a slave or slave-girl. He said: No hadd, no exemplary punishment, nothing; and if the slave-girl marries a free man, likewise there is nothing upon one who slanders a slave-girl or a Christian woman under a Muslim, as hadd, unless the ruler punishes him, unless he sees that."
"13404","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who slanders a slave or slave-girl. He said: He is given ta'zir."
"13405","From Ma'mar, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about a man who slanders a man while drunk. He said: He is subjected to the hadd of slander and the hadd of drunkenness."
"13406","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that a commander among the commanders asked Ibn Umar about a man who slandered a man's umm walad. He said: He is struck the hadd humbled."
"13407","From Umar ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, he said: Ibn Umar was asked about the slanderer of an umm walad, and Ibn Umar said: She is asked about; if no aspersion is cast upon her, her slanderer is subjected to hadd."
"13408","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim and al-Sha'bi, they said: The slanderer of an umm walad is struck. Al-Thawri said: And another said, from al-Sha'bi: When he denies the son of an umm walad from his lineage, saying: You are not your father's, he is struck."
"13409","From Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: When a man says to the son of an umm walad: You are not the son of so-and-so, removing him from his lineage, he is flogged the hadd, even if his mother has not died."
"13410","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Shihab said to me: Concerning an umm walad who commits adultery, and he was asked: Should her master sell her? He said: It is not proper for him to sell her, but the hadd of a slave-girl is established upon her."
"13411","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, he said: When a man says to a man whose mother is an umm walad or a Christian: You are not your father's, he is not struck, because the denial only falls upon the mother; and if a man says to a man: You are not from Banu Tamim, he is not struck, because the denial only falls upon a polytheist. And al-Hakam ibn Utaybah said: He is struck."
"13412","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Ziyad wanted to strike the slanderer of an umm walad, and no one followed him on that."
"13413","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, he said: If a father slanders his son, he is not subjected to hadd."
"13414","He said: And the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Overlook among yourselves, for whatever hadd reaches me has become obligatory."
"13415","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When hudud reach the ruler, it is not lawful for anyone to pardon them. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, the like."
"13416","From al-Thawri, from one who heard al-Hasan and Ata saying: There is no hadd upon a father for his son."
"13417","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: A father is not subjected to retaliation for his son."
"13418","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz handed a man over to his son."
"13419","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ruzayq, the companion of Aylah, informed me that he wrote to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz concerning a man who slandered his son, and he wrote: Subject the father to hadd unless his son pardons him."
"13420","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: Ruzayq informed me, he said: A man slandered his son in my presence, and I wanted to subject him to hadd, and he said: If you subject my father to hadd I will confess, and I do not know what to do, so I wrote about it to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, and he wrote: Subject him to hadd unless he pardons him."
"13421","From Sufyan, concerning a father who slanders his son: As for the son, there is no doubt he is subjected to hadd for his father; as for the father, they prefer averting."
"13422","Sufyan said, concerning a woman who commits adultery and kills her child while not fortified. He said: The hadd is averted from her."
"13423","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar informed me, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: There is no pardon for hudud, for any of them, after they reach the imam, for establishing them is from the Sunnah."
"13424","From Ma'mar, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, he said: Ruzayq informed me that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to him concerning a man who slandered his son: Flog him unless his son pardons him. He said: I thought it was for the father especially, so I wrote to him, and he said: It is for people generally."
"13425","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, that Utbah ibn Abi Waqqas said to his brother Sa'd: Do you know that the child of the slave-girl of Zam'ah is my son? Aishah said: When the day of the Conquest came, Sa'd saw the boy and recognized him by resemblance, so he embraced him to himself, saying: My brother's son, by the Lord of the Ka'bah. Then Abd ibn Zam'ah came and said: Rather he is my brother, born on my father's bed from his slave-girl. They went to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Sa'd said: O Messenger of Allah, my brother's son, look at his resemblance to Utbah. Abd ibn Zam'ah said: Rather he is my brother, born on my father's bed from his slave-girl. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The child belongs to the bed, and veil yourself from him, O Sawdah. Aishah said: By Allah, he never saw her until he died. Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, the like."
"13426","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn al-Zubayr, that Zam'ah had a slave-girl and he used to have intercourse with her, and they used to suspect her, and she gave birth, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to Sawdah: As for inheritance, it is his, but as for you, veil yourself from him, O Sawdah; he is not a brother to you."
"13427","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone."
"13428","From Sufyan, concerning two men disputing over a child born on the bed of one of them. He said: He belongs to the one in whose possession he is, when she gives birth in six months; if less than six months, he belongs to the first, unless it be a day or two. This is concerning a man who sells a slave-girl to a man."
"13429","From Sufyan, concerning a child claimed by two men: he inherits from each of them a full male share, and they both inherit from him a sixth; when one of them dies, he belongs to the remaining one; whoever denies him from one of them is not struck until he denies him from both together; when he passes to the remaining one, he inherits his siblings from the deceased, and they do not inherit him; this living father of his veils him such that the siblings from the deceased do not inherit him while he inherits them, because he is their brother; his inheritance belongs to the remaining one and his blood-money is upon him; when the other of the two fathers dies, his blood-money and inheritance pass to his siblings from both fathers together."
"13430","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas and Abd ibn Zam'ah disputed over a boy. Sa'd said: O Messenger of Allah, my brother Utbah ibn Abi Waqqas enjoined upon me that he is his son; look at his resemblance. Abd ibn Zam'ah said: This is my brother, O Messenger of Allah, born on my father's bed from his slave-girl. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, looked at his resemblance and saw a clear resemblance to Utbah, and said: He is yours, O Abd; the child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone; and veil yourself from him, O Sawdah bint Zam'ah. She said: Sawdah was never seen afterwards."
"13431","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: A man found eating pork saying: I desire it; or a sacrificial camel passed by him and he slaughtered it though he knew it was a sacrificial camel; or a woman broke fast in Ramadan saying: I am menstruating, then women looked at her and she was not menstruating; or a man had intercourse with his wife in Ramadan; or had intercourse with his wife while menstruating; or killed game in the sanctuary deliberately; or drank wine; or left part of the prayer — I mentioned them to him. He said: Allah was not forgetful; had He willed He would have placed something in that, naming it; I have not heard anything about that. Then he returned to saying: If he does that once, there is nothing upon him; if he repeats that, let him be given exemplary punishment. And he mentioned the man who kissed the woman, and I say: the one who had intercourse with his wife in Ramadan."
"13432","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: When he eats pork, repentance is presented to him; if he repents, otherwise he is killed."
"13433","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who breaks fast in Ramadan. He said: If he is a wicked one among the wicked, he is given a painful exemplary punishment, and he also expiates; and if he does that adopting a religion other than Islam, repentance is presented to him."
"13434","From al-Thawri, concerning eating pork. He said: There is no hadd in it, nor ta'zir."
"13435","Isra'il ibn Yunus informed us, from Simak ibn Harb, that he heard Ibrahim narrating from Alqamah and al-Aswad, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: A man came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah! I took a woman in the garden and did everything with her except that I did not have intercourse with her; I kissed her and clung to her and did nothing else. Do with me what you wish. He said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said nothing to him. The man went away, and Umar said: Allah had concealed him, had he concealed himself. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, followed him with his gaze, then said: Bring him back to me. They brought him back, and he recited to him: And establish prayer at the two ends of the day, until: for those who remember. He said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal said: Is it for him alone, O Prophet of Allah, or for people generally? He said: Rather for people generally."
"13436","From Ma'mar, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, I think from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: A man kissed a woman and came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, mentioning to him that he was asking him about its expiation? Umar said: Is she unmarried? He said: Yes. Umar said: I do not know. It is said: The man came to Abu Bakr and mentioned it to him also, and he answered him as he had answered him. Then he came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, asking him, and he said: Is she unmarried? He said: Yes. He said: He was silent about him. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: And establish prayer at the two ends of the day, to: for those who remember."
"13437","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Yahya ibn Ja'dah, that a man among the companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, mentioned a woman while sitting with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and sought permission for a need, and he permitted him. He went seeking her and did not find her. The man came wanting to give glad tidings to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, of rain, and found the woman sitting at a pool; he pushed her chest and sat between her legs, and his member became like a fringe. He stood up remorseful and came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, informing him of what he had done. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: Seek forgiveness of your Lord and pray four rak'ahs. Then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, recited: And establish prayer at the two ends of the day."
"13438","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Bayan, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: A man came pledging allegiance to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, though he had spoken with a woman the day before. He said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, pledged him with his palm, or he said: with the tips of his fingers, and said: You are the companion of yesterday's conversation."
"13439","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Urwah, from Aishah, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered upon her joyful, the lines of his face shining, and said: Have you not heard what Mujazziz al-Mudliji said about Zayd and Usamah? He saw their feet and said: These feet are part of one another. From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, the like, with addition: They were in a blanket, having covered their heads, and their feet appeared, and he did not mention the shining of his face's lines."
"13440","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ziyad, he said: I was with Ibn Abbas, and a man came, I think from Banu Kurz, and saw Ibn Abbas reviling the boy while his mother was reaching for him, and he said: He is your son. He said: Ibn Abbas called him and carried his mother upon his mount, though Ibn Abbas had disowned him."
"13441","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered upon her joyful and said: Have you not heard what al-Mudliji said, seeing Usamah and Zayd sleeping in one garment, or in a blanket, their feet out, saying: These feet are part of one another."
"13442","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab saw a man and said: From whom are you? He said: From Banu so-and-so. He said: Do you have lineage in Najran? He said: No. Umar said: Yes. The man said: No. Umar said: I adjure by Allah any man who knows lineage for this man in Najran to inform us. A man said: I know him, O Commander of the Faithful; a woman from Najran bore him. Umar said: Quiet; we trace footprints."
"13443","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: A man informed me that he came to his family, and they had picked up a foundling; he was taken to Umar and mentioned to him. Umar said: Perhaps the little stranger is a misery, as though suspecting him. The man said: They only picked him up while I was absent. Umar asked about him and he was praised well, so Umar said to him: His wala is yours, and his maintenance is upon us from the treasury. From Ibn Uyaynah the like, except he said: Al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Sunayn Abu Jamilah."
"13444","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abu Jamilah narrated to me that he found a foundling in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab, and came to him, and he suspected him, and he was praised well, so Umar said: He is free, his wala is yours, and his maintenance from the treasury."
"13445","From al-Thawri, from Zuhayr ibn Abi Thabit, from Dhuhl ibn Aws, from Tamim, that he found a foundling and brought him to Ali, and Ali assigned him upon one hundred."
"13446","From al-Thawri, from al-Sha'bi and Ibrahim, concerning the foundling. They said: He is free."
"13447","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, concerning a man with whom is the foundling, then he spends upon him. He said: He has nothing of his maintenance; it is only something counted as devoted for him."
"13448","From Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: If a man picks up a child of adultery and wants to spend upon him while it is a debt upon him, let him call witnesses; and if he wants to count it as devoted for him, let him not call witnesses. Abu Hanifah said: And I say: He has nothing, unless the ruler imposes upon him."
"13449","From al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from al-Hakam, that a woman picked up a boy and spent upon him, then came to Shurayh seeking her maintenance. He said: Nothing for you, and his wala is yours. Sufyan said concerning the inheritance of the foundling: From his companions, it is in the treasury."
"13450","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, he said: The child of adultery who is picked up is either free or a slave of a people; a free man is not enslaved, nor a slave of other people. He denies that he be enslaved. And Amr ibn Dinar said that."
"13451","Ma'mar informed us, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, about the child of adultery picked up? He said: He is free. Ibn Jurayj said: And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz freed them during his caliphate in our land."
"13452","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that a child of adultery — Umar said to a man who picked him up: Have him suckled, yours is his wala, and his suckling from the treasury."
"13453","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, and from Ibn Tawus, from his father, concerning one who claims a child from a slave-girl or a free woman, none disputing him. They said: He does not inherit him; it was fornication."
"13454","From al-Thawri, from Jabbar, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Claiming a child of adultery is not permitted in Islam."
"13455","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever commits fornication with a free woman or with a people's slave-girl, the child is a child of adultery; he neither inherits nor is inherited from."
"13456","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ya'qub ibn Ata, he said: I heard Amr ibn Shu'ayb saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever commits fornication with a people's slave-girl or commits adultery with a free woman, the child is a child of adultery; he neither inherits nor is inherited from."
"13457","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I asked Ibn Tawus: What did your father say about a child of adultery? His master frees him, then his father claims him, and his freedmen leave him between him and his father? He said: He used to say: He does not inherit."
"13458","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata was asked about a child born to a slave-girl, freed by the lady's masters, then his father claimed him, and his freedmen recognized he is his son, then he died — does his father inherit him? He said: The child of adultery: Yes. And Amr ibn Dinar, from Ma'mar or another, narrates from al-Hasan, like the statement of Ata."
"13459","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: If his freedmen recognize he is his son and they dispute him over his inheritance. He said: His father inherits him when they recognize he is his son; but if they deny he is his son, his inheritance belongs to them."
"13460","Sufyan said: Concerning the inheritance of the foundling, from his companions, he said: It is in the treasury."
"13461","Ibn Jurayj and Ma'mar informed us, they said: Ibn Tawus informed us that his father used to say a severe statement concerning associating with the child of adultery."
"13462","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Jaban, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There shall not enter Paradise one disobedient to his parents, nor an addict of wine, nor a remindful bestower, nor a child of adultery."
"13463","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, that when it was said to her: He is the worst of the three, she disapproved of that and said: There is no sin of his parents upon him; Allah said: No bearer of burdens shall bear the burden of another."
"13464","From al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, she said: What sin of his parents is upon him? Allah said: No bearer of burdens shall bear the burden of another."
"13465","From Abu Ma'shar, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b, from Maymun ibn Mihran, that he witnessed Ibn Umar pray over a child of adultery, and said to him: Abu Hurayrah did not pray over him, saying: He is the worst of the three. Ibn Umar said to him: He is the best of the three."
"13466","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Hazim informed me, from Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, that he said: He is the best of the three, for the son."
"13467","Abd al-Karim informed me, he said: The father of a child of adultery, that being known, would often pass by the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and people would say: He is an evil man! So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: He is the best of the three, for the father. People changed it, saying: The child is the worst of the three."
"13468","Al-Thawri informed us, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Umar said: A claim for a child of adultery is not permitted in Islam."
"13469","From Ibn al-Taymi, he said: Khalid al-Raba'i narrated to us, he said: And he was with us like Wahb with you; in some books, that he read in some books: The child of adultery shall not enter Paradise until seven, then Allah lightened for this ummah, making it to five fathers."
"13470","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: It reached me that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to say: That I be carried upon two sandals in the path of Allah is more beloved to me than freeing a child of adultery."
"13471","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata used to command freeing him and caring for him, meaning the child of adultery."
"13472","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that al-Zubayr ibn Musa ibn Mina informed him that Umm Salih bint Alqamah ibn al-Murtafi' informed her that she asked Aishah, Mother of the Believers, about freeing children of adultery? She said: Free them and do good to them. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from al-Zubayr ibn Musa, from Umm Hakim bint Tariq, from Aishah, the like."
"13473","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amr also informed me that Sulayman ibn Yasar informed him that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to enjoin good for children of adultery."
"13474","Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that he would free the child of adultery, volunteering it."
"13475","From Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar freed a child of adultery and his mother."
"13476","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, from Salim, he said: Ibn Umar freed a child of adultery and his mother."
"13477","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said concerning children of adultery: Free them and do good to them."
"13478","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar freed a prostitute and her son."
"13479","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Mujahid, concerning the child of adultery. He said: He does not free him, nor buy him, nor eat his price."
"13480","From Umar ibn Rashid, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, that a man narrated to him that a freedwoman of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, narrated to him that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave her a slave-girl, and that slave-girl gave birth from adultery, so she asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about freeing that child of hers? The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to her: That you give charity is better than freeing her."
"13481","Yahya ibn Abi Kathir said: And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz would not permit the testimony of the child of adultery."
"13482","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Abd al-Karim, that Nafi' said: Ibn Umar freed a child of adultery."
"13483","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ibn al-Munkadir, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Honor him and do good to him, meaning the child of adultery."
"13484","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said concerning children of adultery: Free them and do good to them."
"13485","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Ata being asked; a man said to him: A woman gave me her milk to drink after I had become a grown man; may I marry her? He said: No. I said: Is that your opinion? He said: Yes. Ata said: Aishah used to command the daughters of her brothers with that."
"13486","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abdullah ibn Ubaydillah ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me that al-Qasim ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr informed him that Aishah informed him that Sahlah bint Suhayl ibn Amr came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, Salim, the freedman of Abu Hudhayfah, is with us in our house, and he has reached what men reach and knows what men know. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Breastfeed him, you will become forbidden to him. Ibn Abi Mulaykah said: I waited a year or nearly a year without narrating it out of fear of it, then I met al-Qasim and said: You narrated to me a hadith you did not narrate afterwards. He said: What is it? So I told him, and he said: Narrate it from me, that Aishah informed me of it."
"13487","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: Sahlah bint Suhayl ibn Amr came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: Salim used to be attributed to Abu Hudhayfah, and Allah Almighty revealed in His Book: Call them by their fathers, and he used to enter upon me while I was in light clothing, and we are in a narrow house. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Breastfeed Salim, you will become forbidden to him. Al-Zuhri said: Some of the wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: We do not know, perhaps this was a concession for Salim specifically. Al-Zuhri said: And Aishah used to give ruling that breastfeeding after weaning makes forbidden, until she died."
"13488","From Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, that Abu Hudhayfah ibn Utbah ibn Rabiah, and he was a Badr veteran, had adopted Salim, who is called Salim the freedman of Abu Hudhayfah, just as the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had adopted Zayd. And Abu Hudhayfah married Salim, while considering him his son, to his brother's daughter Fatimah bint al-Walid ibn Utbah, and she was among the first emigrant women, and at that time among the best unmarried women of Quraysh. When Allah Almighty revealed what He revealed: Call them by their fathers, each one of them was returned to his father, and if his father was not known he was returned to his patrons. Then Sahlah bint Suhayl, wife of Abu Hudhayfah, from Banu Amir ibn Luayy, came and said: O Messenger of Allah, we used to consider Salim as a child, and he used to enter upon me while I was in light clothing, and we have only one room, so what do you think? Al-Zuhri said: He said to her, in what reached us, and Allah knows best: Breastfeed him five nursings, so you become forbidden through her milk. And she used to consider him a son through breastfeeding. So Aishah adopted that for men she wanted to enter upon her, she used to order Umm Kulthum bint Abi Bakr and her brother's daughters to nurse for her whomever she loved to enter upon her of men. And the rest of the wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, refused that anyone enter upon them through that nursing. They said: By Allah, we do not see what the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered Sahlah except a concession for Salim alone."
"13489","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, he said: Urwah informed me, from Aishah, that Abu Hudhayfah adopted Salim, the freedman of a woman of the Ansar, just as the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, adopted Zayd. And whoever adopted a man in the pre-Islamic age, people called him his son and he inherited from his inheritance, until Allah Almighty revealed: Call them by their fathers, that is more just with Allah; if you do not know their fathers, then your brothers in religion. So they were returned to their fathers, and whoever had no known father became a patron and brother in religion. Then Sahlah came and said: O Messenger of Allah, we used to consider Salim a child living with me and with Abu Hudhayfah, and he sees me in light clothing, and Allah Almighty has revealed concerning him what You know. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Breastfeed him five nursings, and he was in the position of her child through breastfeeding."
"13490","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me that Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, the freedman of al-Ashjai, informed him, and Mujahid, that his father informed him that he asked Ali, saying: I wanted to marry a woman who gave me her milk to drink when I was grown, as medicine. Ali said: Do not marry her, and he forbade him from her."
"13491","That he said, from Ali also, he used to say: If his wife gave him to drink from the milk of his concubine, or his concubine from the milk of his wife, to make her forbidden to him, that does not make her forbidden."
"13492","Ibn Jurayj informed me, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdillah say: A man came to Umar ibn al-Khattab and said: My wife nursed my concubine to make her forbidden to me. So Umar ordered the wife to be flogged, and that he go to his concubine after the nursing."
"13493","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that a woman nursed her husband's slave girl to make her forbidden to him. Umar was approached and that was mentioned to him, so he said: I adjure you to return and pain your wife's back and have intercourse with your slave girl."
"13494","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed that Umar ibn al-Khattab was approached by a Bedouin who said: My wife said: Relieve me of some of my milk. He said: I fear it will make you forbidden to me. She said: No. So he relieved her, and did not let it enter his stomach, though he found its sweetness in his throat. Then she said: Know, you have become forbidden to me. So Umar said: She is your wife, so strike her."
"13495","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached me that a man of the Ansar of Banu Harithah had a slave girl he had intercourse with. One day he went out to pray with Umar ibn al-Khattab, and his wife nursed his slave girl, forcing her. That was reported to Umar, so Umar said: You shall return to your slave girl and have intercourse with her, and you shall pain your wife's back. And his name is Isa ibn Hazm ibn Amr ibn Zayd ibn Harithah."
"13496","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I sent a man to Ata about nose-drops of milk for a child and kohl with it, does it make forbidden? He said: We never heard that it makes forbidden."
"13497","Al-Thawri informed us, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Shabi, he said: Every nose-drop, or forced drink, or nursing nursed before the two years, it makes forbidden; and what is after the two years does not make forbidden. Abd al-Razzaq said: And the people follow this."
"13498","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Atiyyah al-Wadii, he said: A man came to Ibn Masud and said: My wife was with me, her milk became confined in her breast, so I began sucking it then spitting it out. Then I went to Abu Musa and asked him, and he said: She has become forbidden to you. He said: So he stood and we stood with him until he reached Abu Musa, and said: What did you rule for this one? He told him what he had ruled. Then Ibn Masud took my hand and said: Do you see this one as an infant! Breastfeeding is only what grows flesh and blood. So Abu Musa said: Do not ask me about anything while this scholar is among you."
"13499","From Mamar, from Qatadah: By Allah, I will not give you a ruling while it is with her."
"13500","From Mamar, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, from al-Nazzal, from Ali, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: No breastfeeding after weaning."
"13501","From al-Thawri, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak, from al-Nazzal, from Ali, he said: No breastfeeding after weaning. And I heard him say to Mamar: He did not raise it to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Mamar said: Yes he did."
"13502","Mamar informed us, from Haram ibn Uthman, from Abd al-Rahman and Muhammad the two sons of Jabir, from their father Jabir ibn Abdillah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: No oath for a child along with the oath of a parent, no oath for a wife along with the oath of a husband, no oath for a slave along with the oath of an owner, no oath in severing kinship, no vow in disobedience, no divorce before marriage, no emancipation before ownership, no silence for a day until night, no continuous fasting without break, no orphanhood after puberty, no breastfeeding after weaning, no returning to Bedouin life after migration, and no migration after the Conquest."
"13503","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that Ibn Umar, or Ibn Abbas, said: No breastfeeding after weaning within the two years."
"13504","From Mamar, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Ibn Abbas said: No breastfeeding after weaning at two years."
"13505","From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Dinar, from one who heard Ibn Abbas say: No breastfeeding after weaning."
"13506","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Ibn Abbas used to say: No breastfeeding except what is within the two years."
"13507","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: I do not know breastfeeding except what is in childhood."
"13508","From Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he said: No breastfeeding except for one nursed in childhood, and no breastfeeding for an adult."
"13509","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar used to say: We do not know breastfeeding except what is nursed in childhood."
"13510","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Said, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, he said: No breastfeeding except what is in the cradle."
"13511","From Mamar, from al-Hasan, al-Zuhri, and Qatadah, they said: No breastfeeding after weaning."
"13512","From Mamar, from one who heard Ikrimah say: Breastfeeding after weaning is like flowing water he drinks."
"13513","Ibn Jurayj and Mamar informed us, they said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from Urwah, from al-Hajjaj ibn al-Hajjaj al-Aslami, that he sought a ruling from Abu Hurayrah, saying: Nothing makes forbidden except what opens the intestines."
"13514","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata said: What is little and what is much of it makes forbidden."
"13515","He said: And Ibn Umar said, when it reached him from Ibn al-Zubayr that he reports from Aishah concerning breastfeeding, that he said: Nothing less than seven nursings makes forbidden. He said: Allah is better than Aishah. Allah Almighty said: And your sisters through breastfeeding, and He did not say one nursing nor two."
"13516","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Aishah, she said: Nothing less than five known nursings makes forbidden."
"13517","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amrah, from Aishah, she said: The Quran was revealed with ten known nursings, then they became five."
"13518","From Mamar, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, he said: The wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had known nursings. He said: Then that was abandoned afterwards, so little and much of it made forbidden."
"13519","From Mamar, that the wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when they nursed an adult he entered upon them, and that was for the wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, specifically; for other people it is only what is in childhood."
"13520","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me, from Tawus, he said: I said to him: They claim that nothing less than seven nursings makes forbidden of breastfeeding, then that became five. So Tawus said: That did exist, then afterwards a matter came: prohibition came with a single time making forbidden."
"13521","From al-Thawri and Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from Tawus, he said: A single time of breastfeeding makes forbidden."
"13522","Ibn Jurayj informed me, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, that he said: A single time makes forbidden. I said: Is it the suck? He said: Yes."
"13523","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that he heard Ibn Umar, when a man asked him: Does one nursing or two make forbidden? He said: We do not know a sister through breastfeeding except as forbidden. Then a man said: The Commander of the Believers, meaning Ibn al-Zubayr, claims that one nursing nor two make forbidden. So Ibn Umar said: The decree of Allah is better than your decree and the decree of the Commander of the Believers. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar and Ibn al-Zubayr similarly."
"13524","From Mamar, from Ibrahim ibn Uqbah, he said: I went to Urwah ibn al-Zubayr and asked him about an infant who drank a little of a woman's milk. Urwah said to me: Aishah used to say: Nothing less than seven nursings, or five, makes forbidden. He said: So I went to Ibn al-Musayyab and asked him. He said: I do not say the saying of Aishah, nor the saying of Ibn Abbas, but if a drop entered his stomach after knowing it entered his stomach, he becomes forbidden."
"13525","From Mamar, from Ayyub, that Ibn al-Zubayr used to say: One suck and two sucks do not make forbidden. Ibn al-Zubayr narrates that from Aishah."
"13526","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, from one who heard al-Hasan, they said concerning breastfeeding: Its little and much are the same."
"13527","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Ali and Ibn Masud, they said concerning breastfeeding: Its little and much make forbidden. So I narrated to Mamar, and he said: He spoke the truth."
"13528","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from Urwah, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, that he narrated from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he said: One suck of breastfeeding and not two sucks make forbidden."
"13529","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abi al-Khalil, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Umm al-Fadl, that a woman was divorced by her husband, then the man married another woman, and he claimed that his wife nursed her. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: One gulp and not two gulps make forbidden."
"13530","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that Aishah ordered Umm Kulthum to nurse Salim, so she nursed him five nursings, then she fell ill, so Salim would not enter upon Aishah."
"13531","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Nafi narrate that Salim ibn Abdillah narrated to him that Aishah, wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent him to her sister Umm Kulthum bint Abi Bakr to nurse him ten nursings so he could enter upon her when grown. So she nursed him three times, then she fell ill, so Salim would not enter upon her. He said: They claimed that Aishah said: There was in the Book of Allah Almighty: ten nursings, then that was reduced to five; but part of the Book of Allah was taken with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"13532","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Nafi, freedman of Ibn Umar, narrate that the daughter of Abi Ubayd, wife of Ibn Umar, informed him that Hafsah bint Umar, wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent a precious boy of some patrons of Umar to her sister Fatimah bint Umar, ordering her to nurse him ten times. So she did, and he used to enter upon her after he grew up. Ibn Jurayj said: And I was informed that his name is Asim ibn Abdillah ibn Sad, freedman of Umar; Musa informed me of it from Nafi."
"13533","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ibn Ajlan, he said: I was informed that Umar was brought a boy and a girl whom they wanted to marry to each other, and they were informed that one of them had nursed. He said: How did the other nurse? He said: She passed by him while he was crying and nursed him, or gave him to suck. So he raised them with the stick, then said: Marry them to each other, for breastfeeding is only nurturing."
"13534","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Thawr, from Amr ibn Shuayb, that Sufyan ibn Abdillah wrote to Umar asking him what makes forbidden of breastfeeding. So he wrote to him: Nothing of it makes forbidden by trickery, scanty remains, or a snatched gulp. Trickery is that she nurses two children so prohibition comes between them; scanty is the little thing remaining in the breast; and the snatched gulp is a woman snatching another's child and giving him her breast."
"13535","Mamar and Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he said: The milk of the father does not make forbidden, and he used to call it the sire's milk."
"13536","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: Does the sire's milk make forbidden? He said: Yes. Allah said: And your sisters through breastfeeding, so she is your sister from your father."
"13537","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that he heard Aba al-Shatha consider the sire's milk as making forbidden."
"13538","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, that he used to dislike the sire's milk."
"13539","From al-Thawri, from Abbad ibn Mansur, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad and al-Hasan, that they also disliked the sire's milk."
"13540","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: Aflah, brother of Abi al-Quays, came seeking permission to enter upon her, saying: I am her uncle. But she refused to permit him. When the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered upon her, she mentioned that to him. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Why did you not permit your uncle? She said: O Messenger of Allah, only the woman nursed me, the man did not nurse me. He said: Permit him, for he is your uncle, may your right hand be dusted. He said: And Abu al-Quays was the husband of the woman who nursed Aishah. From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah similarly."
"13541","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, he said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that Aishah informed him, she said: My uncle through breastfeeding, Abu al-Jad, sought permission to enter upon me and I turned him away. Ibn Jurayj said: Hisham said to me: He is only Abu al-Quays. When the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came, I told him of that. He said: Why did you not permit him, may your right hand be dusted, or he said: your hand."
"13542","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Hisham informed me, from his father, from Aishah, she said: My uncle through breastfeeding came after the veil was imposed upon me and sought permission to enter upon me. So I said: By Allah, I will not permit you until the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, comes and I seek his permission. He said to her: Let your uncle enter upon you. She said: Only the woman nursed me, the man did not nurse me. He said: He is only your uncle, so let him enter upon you. From al-Thawri, from Hisham, from Urwah, from Aishah similarly, and al-Thawri holds by this."
"13543","From Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Amr ibn al-Sharid, he said: Ibn Abbas was asked about a man who married two women, one nursed a girl and the other nursed a boy: can the boy marry the girl? He said: No, the fecundation is one; she is not lawful to him."
"13544","From al-Thawri, from Khusayf, from Salim ibn Abdillah, from Ibn Umar, he said: There is no harm in the sire's milk. Muhammad said: And Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed me, from a man, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, that he said: There is no harm in it."
"13545","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no harm in it."
"13546","And his saying: What is forbidden through breastfeeding is forbidden as through lineage: if a slave girl drank your mother's milk with you, she is not lawful to you nor to any of your brothers. But if you nursed another's milk with a slave girl, she is lawful to your brother if your brother did not nurse from her mother."
"13547","From al-Thawri, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Judan, from Ibn al-Musayyab, from Ali, he said: I said to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: Shall I not point you to the most beautiful girl of Quraysh? He said: Who is she? I said: The daughter of Hamzah. He said: She is the daughter of my brother through breastfeeding. Do you not know that Allah forbade through breastfeeding what He forbade through lineage."
"13548","Ibn Jurayj and Mamar informed us, they said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from Urwah, from Zaynab bint Abi Salamah, from Umm Habibah, she said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, entered upon me, and I said: Would you like my sister, the daughter of Abu Sufyan? He said: Do what? I said: Marry her. He said: Your sister? She said: Yes. He said: And do you love that? She said: Yes, I am not alone with you, and I love, or she said: and the most entitled to share with me in good is my sister. He said: She is not lawful to me. She said: By Allah, I have been told that you are proposing Durrah bint Abi Salamah. He said: The daughter of Umm Salamah? She said: Yes. He said: By Allah, if she were not my stepdaughter in my care she would not be lawful to me. She is the daughter of my brother through breastfeeding; Thuwaybah nursed me and her father. So do not offer me your daughters nor your sisters."
"13549","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir and Jabir al-Jufi, from Ikrimah, he said: The daughter of Hamzah was offered to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he said: She is the daughter of my brother through breastfeeding."
"13550","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, she said: What is forbidden through breastfeeding is forbidden as through birth."
"13551","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, that he used to say: What is forbidden through breastfeeding is forbidden as through lineage."
"13552","From Israil ibn Yunus, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: What is forbidden through breastfeeding is forbidden as through lineage."
"13553","Ibn Jurayj and Ibrahim informed us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Amrah, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: What is forbidden through breastfeeding is forbidden as through birth."
"13554","From Umar ibn Habib, he said: A shaykh narrated to me, he said: I sat with Ibn Umar, and he said: Are you of the sons of so-and-so? I said: No, but they nursed me. He said: I heard Umar say: Milk resembles him."
"13555","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Muslim ibn Abi Maryam informed me, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, that she used to say: What is forbidden through breastfeeding is forbidden as through birth."
"13556","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me, from Zaynab bint Abi Salamah, that Umm Habibah, wife of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: O Messenger of Allah, marry my sister, the daughter of Abu Sufyan. So the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to her: Do you love that? She said: Yes, and I am not alone with you, and the best who shares with me in good is my sister. He said: That is not lawful. She said: By Allah, we talk that you want to marry Durrah bint Abi Salamah. He said: The daughter of Umm Salamah? She said: I said: Yes. He said: By Allah, if she were not my stepdaughter she would not be lawful to me. She is the daughter of my brother through breastfeeding; Thuwaybah nursed me and her father. So do not offer me your daughters and sisters. Urwah said: Thuwaybah was a freedwoman of Abu Lahab; Abu Lahab had freed her, and she nursed the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. When Abu Lahab died, some of his family saw him in a dream and said to him: What did you meet? Or he said: find? Abu Lahab said: I did not meet or find ease after you, or he said: rest, except that I was given drink in this part of me for freeing Thuwaybah, and he pointed to the hollow next to the thumb and the one next to it."
"13557","From Mamar, Ibn Jurayj, and al-Thawri, they said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from al-Hajjaj al-Aslami, from his father, that he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what removes the blame of breastfeeding? He said: A fine slave or slave girl. Mamar said: And she has afterwards a right in kindness."
"13558","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: I asked him about a nursing woman with the milk of an illegitimate child? He said: There is no harm in it; the Jewess, Christian, and Magian woman may nurse the Muslim. Ibrahim said: And they used to like that something be given to the wet nurse at weaning."
"13559","From Abu Bakr ibn Abi Sabrah, from Ibrahim ibn Abdillah, from Abdullah ibn Abdillah ibn Utbah, from one of the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: The Sadite sister of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came to him on his return from Hunayn. When he saw her he welcomed her and spread his cloak for her to sit on. She deemed that great, so he insisted to her, and she sat. Then the eyes of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, shed tears until his beard was wetted with his tears. A man of the people said: Do you weep, O Messenger of Allah! He said: Yes, out of mercy for her and what befell her. If one of you had Uhud in gold and gave it for the right of his nursing, he would not fulfill her right. As for my right which I take from you, it is yours; as for what belongs to the Muslims, I will not take it except if they give it willingly. She said: No one of the Muslims remained except he gave her what he had taken from her."
"13560","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, al-Hasan, and Qatadah, they saw no harm in a man marrying the daughter of a woman his father had relations with."
"13561","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata used to say: A man divorced a woman, then she married a man and bore him a girl, and the first husband had a son. He said: There is no harm in his son marrying the daughter of his wife from the man she married after him."
"13562","From al-Thawri, he said: There is no harm in it. And Layth mentioned, from Mujahid, that he used to dislike it, but that did not please us."
"13563","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to dislike a man marrying the daughter of a woman his father had intercourse with: whatever child she bore before his father had intercourse with her, there is no harm in marrying her; whatever child she bore after his father had intercourse with her, he shall not marry any of her children. From Mamar, he said: I said to Ibn Abi Najih: Do you know anyone who dislikes that? He said: Mujahid used to dislike it. Mamar said: I found no one who disliked it except what was mentioned from Tawus and Mujahid."
"13564","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: There is no harm in a man marrying the daughter of a man and his wife, if his daughter is from another woman."
"13565","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abdullah ibn Jafar combined the wife of Ali and her daughter from another man, marrying them both together."
"13566","From al-Thawri, when asked about a man marrying a man's wife and his daughter, combining them, from another man, he said: There is no harm in that, and some eminent one did it."
"13567","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me that Uqbah ibn al-Harith ibn Amir informed him, or he heard it from him if he did not specify it to him: that he married Umm Yahya bint Abi Ihab, and a black woman said: I nursed you both. He said: So I went to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and mentioned that to him. So he turned away from me. Then I went to him a second time and mentioned that to him, and he said: How, when she claimed she nursed you both? So he forbade him from her."
"13568","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Ubayd ibn Abi Maryam, from Uqbah ibn al-Harith. Ibn Abi Mulaykah said: And I heard it from Uqbah also, he said: I married a woman in the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and a black woman came claiming she nursed us both. He said: So I brought her to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and mentioned that to him, saying: She is lying. But he turned away from me. Then I moved to the other side and said: O Messenger of Allah, she is lying. He said: What will you do with the saying of this one? Leave her. Mamar said: And I heard another say: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: How about you, when it has been said?"
"13569","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that Uthman separated the people of households by the testimony of a woman."
"13570","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ibn Shihab, he said: A black woman came in the reign of Uthman to the people of three households who had intermarried, and said: You are my sons and daughters. So they were separated."
"13571","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Abi al-Shatha, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The testimony of a single woman is allowed in breastfeeding if she is trustworthy, and she is made to swear along with her testimony. He said: A man came to Ibn Abbas and said: So-and-so claimed she nursed me and my wife, and she is lying. So Ibn Abbas said: Look, if she is lying she will be struck by affliction. He said: A year did not pass before her breast became leprous."
"13572","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The testimony of women is allowed for everything that none but they see, and no fewer than four women of them is allowed."
"13573","From Mamar, from Qatadah, he said: Their testimony is not allowed unless they are four."
"13574","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, and from a man, from al-Hasan, they said: The testimony of a single trustworthy woman is allowed in breastfeeding and childbirth."
"13575","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The testimony of a single woman is allowed in breastfeeding. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Tawus, from his father similarly, adding: Even if she is black."
"13576","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, he said: Judges used to separate by the testimony of a woman in breastfeeding."
"13577","From al-Thawri, he said: Ashath informed me, from al-Shabi: The testimony of a single woman is allowed in what men do not see. From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Hasan, like the saying of al-Shabi."
"13578","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, he said: Two women."
"13579","From al-Thawri, from Zayd ibn Aslam, that Umar did not take the testimony of a woman in breastfeeding. He said: And Ibn Abi Layla did not take the testimony of a woman in breastfeeding."
"13580","From a shaykh of Najran, he said: I heard Ibn al-Baylamani narrate, from his father, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was asked: What witnesses are allowed in breastfeeding? He said: A man or a woman."
"13581","From Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Shubah, from Abi al-Bakhtari, he said: I heard al-Shabi say: The testimony of women is allowed for what men do not see, four. Shubah said: And I heard al-Hakam say: Two. And I asked Hammad, and he said: One."
"13582","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: One."
"13583","From Abu Bakr ibn Abi Sabrah, from Abi al-Zinad, Yahya ibn Rabiah, that the testimony of a single woman, if trustworthy and heard from her before the marriage, is allowed alone in breastfeeding and the newborn's cry."
"13584","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Abdullah ibn Nujayy, from Ali, and from Abd al-Ala from Shurayh, and from Hammad from Ibrahim, that they allowed the testimony of a single woman in the newborn's cry."
"13585","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Anbasah, freedman of Talhah ibn Dawud, informed me that he heard Talhah ibn Dawud say: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: What good nursers are the people of Oman."
"13586","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Nawfal ibn Anas informed me that his mother nursed Umm Salamah bint Hamzah ibn Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr. She said: She brought her to Asma bint Abi Bakr and said: Whose daughter are you, little girl? She said: From Hudhayl. She said: Abu Bakr said: The best wet nurses that burdened the necks of camels are the women of Hudhayl."
"13587","Ibn Jurayj informed us, Ata said, concerning intercourse with a nursing woman: It occurred to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he forbade it, then said: If it were harmful it would have harmed Rome and Persia."
"13588","From al-Thawri, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, he said: A man came complaining of his wife to Ibn al-Musayyab. So Ibn al-Musayyab said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Any woman who is not independent of her husband and is not grateful to him, Allah Almighty will not look at her on the Day of Resurrection. Then a man with Ibn al-Musayyab said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whichever woman her husband swears a true oath upon and she does not fulfill it, seventy prayers are deducted from her. He said: Then another man with Ibn al-Musayyab said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Any woman who attaches to a people a lineage not of them, her weight on the Day of Resurrection will not equal a mote's weight."
"13589","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Sharik ibn Abi Namir, from Umar ibn al-Hakam ibn Thawban, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever bequeaths wealth to other than its people, upon her is half the punishment of the community."
"13590","Ismail informed me that Aishah used to forbid a married woman from leaving her legs bare without anything on them, and she used to say: A woman should not abandon dye, for the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to dislike masculinity in women."
"13591","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from Fatimah bint al-Mundhir, that she said: I never saw Asma wear except dyed with safflower until she met Allah, and she used to wear a tunic standing stiff from safflower dye."
"13592","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Haram ibn Utlah informed me that his maternal aunt informed him that she saw Aishah, Mother of the Believers, dyed with henna, wearing stained garments. He said: And I saw Safiyyah bint Shaybah dyed with henna, wearing safflower-dyed garments."
"13593","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, Aishah bint Abi Bakr, Umm Salamah bint Abi Umayyah, Hafsah bint Umar, Umm Habibah bint Abi Sufyan, Juwayriyah bint al-Harith, Maymunah bint al-Harith, Zaynab bint Jahsh, Sawdah bint Zamah, and Safiyyah bint Huyayy. Nine gathered with him after Khadijah; and the Kindite woman of Banu al-Jawn, al-Aliyah bint Zabyan of Banu Amir ibn Kilab, and Zaynab bint Khuzaymah, a woman of Banu Hilal."
"13594","Mamar said, and al-Zuhri informed me, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr: When the Kindite woman entered upon the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, she said: I seek refuge in Allah from you! So he said: You have sought refuge in a Mighty One; join your family."
"13595","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, divorced al-Aliyah bint Zabyan, and a cousin of hers married her, and that was before their marriage to people became forbidden; and she bore him a child."
"13596","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: The first woman the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, married was Khadijah, then he married Sawdah bint Zamah, then he wed Aishah in Makkah and consummated with her in Madinah, and he wed in Madinah Zaynab bint Khuzaymah al-Hilaliyyah, then he wed Umm Salamah, then he wed Juwayriyah bint al-Harith, and she was of what Allah granted him, then he wed Maymunah bint al-Harith, and she is the one who offered herself to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, then he wed Safiyyah bint Huyayy, and she was of what Allah granted him on the day of Khaybar, then he wed Zaynab bint Jahsh, and she was the wife of Zayd ibn Harithah. And Zaynab bint Khuzaymah died with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Khadijah also died in Makkah. And he wed a woman of Banu Kilab ibn Rabiah called al-Aliyah bint Zabyan, and divorced her when she was brought to him. And Juwayriyah is of Banu al-Mustaliq of Khuzah; and Hafsah, Umm Habibah, and a woman of Kalb. The total he married was fourteen, among them the Kindite woman."
"13597","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata and Amr, they said: They gathered with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he had ordered the veil to be drawn over Safiyyah: Khadijah, Aishah, Umm Salamah, Hafsah, Umm Habibah, Juwayriyah al-Mustaliqiyyah, Maymunah, Zaynab bint Jahsh of Banu Asad among Banu Harb, Sawdah of Banu Amir ibn Luayy, and Safiyyah bint Huyayy."
"13598","From al-Mujalid, from a man, from al-Shabi, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, married a woman of Kindah, and she was brought after the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had died."
"13599","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah and Amr said: Nine women gathered with the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, after Khadijah, and he died leaving all of them. He said: And Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman added: Two women besides the nine of Banu Amir ibn Sasah, both he consummated with; one of them was called Umm al-Masakin, and she was the best of his wives to the poor. And he wed a woman of Banu al-Jawn; when she came to him she sought refuge from him, so he divorced her. And he wed another woman of Kindah and did not consummate with her; she married after the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, so Umar separated them and struck her husband. She said: Fear Allah concerning me, O Umar; if I am of the Mothers of the Believers, draw the veil over me and give me like you gave them. He said: As for that, no. She said: Then let me marry. He said: No, nor the delight of the eye, and I will obey no one in that."
"13600","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, that Aishah said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not die until it was made lawful for him to marry whatever he wished. I said: From whom do you report this? I said: I do not know; I thought I heard a servant say that. He said: And Amr said to me: I heard Ata some time ago say: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not die until it was made lawful for him to marry whatever he wished."
"13601","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, died, and we do not know him marrying women."
"13602","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: Khadijah died three years before the departure of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, or thereabouts, and he wed Aishah close to the death of Khadijah; and he did not marry over Khadijah until she died."
"13603","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan say: When the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave his wives the choice, they chose, choosing Allah and His Messenger, so he kept them. Then Allah said: Women are not lawful to you after this, the verse."
"13604","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: I know only that he informed me, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, had two concubines: the Copt and Rayhanah bint Shamun."
"13605","Mamar and Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Jafar, that Ali ibn Abi Talib said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: The best of its women is Maryam, and the best of its women is Khadijah bint Khuwaylid."
"13606","From Mamar, he said: I heard al-Zuhri say: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not marry over Khadijah until she died. And Aishah said: I never saw Khadijah at all, and I never envied a woman as intensely as my envy of Khadijah, and that was from how much he used to mention her."
"13607","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata informed me that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, did not marry over Khadijah until she died."
"13608","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Khadijah bore the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: al-Qasim, Tahir, Fatimah, Zaynab, Umm Kulthum, and Ruqayyah. Al-Zuhri said: And men of the scholars say: We do not know Khadijah bore him a male except al-Qasim."
"13609","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Copt bore him; al-Zuhri said: And no woman of his wives bore him except Khadijah."
"13610","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: More than one said to me: Khadijah bore him four daughters, and Abdullah and al-Qasim; and the Copt bore him Ibrahim. And Zaynab was the eldest of the daughters of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Fatimah was the youngest of them and most beloved to him. And he left her with Umm Hani; and Ali and Uthman married in Islam, and Zaynab married in the pre-Islamic age."
"13611","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Mujahid said, he said: Al-Qasim, son of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, lived seven nights, then died."
"13612","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abi al-Duha, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: Ibrahim, son of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, died at sixteen months. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Bury him in al-Baqi, for he has a wet nurse completing his nursing in Paradise."
"13613","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, prayed over the son of Mariyah the Copt, at sixteen months."
"13614","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade a man visiting his family at night after the night prayer."
"13615","From Ubaydillah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: He returned from a campaign, and when he reached al-Jurf he said: Do not come to women at night, nor deceive them. And he sent a rider to Madinah informing them that the people enter in the morning."
"13616","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, upon his arrival from Syria, sent Aslam his freedman to the people of Madinah informing them: We are coming to you on such-and-such."
"13617","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, he said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, camped at al-Muarras, he ordered a caller who called: Do not come to women at night. He said: Two men hastened, and each found a man with his wife. That was mentioned to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he said: I forbade you from coming to women at night."
"13618","From Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, that Ibn Rawahah was in a detachment and returned, coming to his house girt with a sword, and there was the lamp. He became suspicious and climbed over, and there was his wife lying on a bed, beside her, as it seemed, a man with disheveled head hair. He meant to strike him, then piety restrained him. So he nudged his wife and she woke, saying: Back, back. He said: Woe to you, who is this? She said: This is my sister, she stayed with me and washed her head. When that reached the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he forbade visiting women at night. Then two men disobeyed him and visited their families at night, each finding a man with his wife. When that reached the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: Did I not forbid you from visiting women at night."
"13619","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym informed me, he said: There was in Makkah a beautiful devout Iraqi woman with a son called Abu Umayyah, and Said ibn Jubayr used to visit her often. I said: O Abu Abdillah! How often you enter upon this woman. He said: We married her that marriage for enjoyment. He said: And he informed me that Said said to him: She is more lawful than drinking water, for enjoyment."
"13620","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: The first I heard temporary marriage from was Safwan ibn Yala. He said: Yala informed me that Muawiyah enjoyed a woman at Taif, and she disapproved of that to him. So we entered upon Ibn Abbas, and one of us mentioned it to him. So he said to him: Yes. But it did not settle in my mind until Jabir ibn Abdillah arrived, and we went to him in his house. The people asked him about things, then mentioned temporary marriage to him. So he said: Yes, we enjoyed in the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Abu Bakr and Umar, until at the end of Umar's caliphate, Amr ibn Hurayth enjoyed a woman Jabir named but I forgot. The woman became pregnant, and that reached Umar. So he called her and asked her. She said: Yes. He said: Who witnessed? Ata said: I do not know, she said: My mother, the mother of her guardian. He said: Why not others besides them? He said: He feared the other might be deceit. Ata said: And I heard Ibn Abbas say: May Allah have mercy on Umar; temporary marriage was only a concession from Allah Almighty, with which He showed mercy to the community of Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. Had he not forbidden it, none would need adultery except a wretch. He said: As if I hear his saying now: except a wretch, Ata is the speaker. Ata said: It is the one in Surah al-Nisa: Whatever you enjoy of them, to such-and-such of term for such-and-such, without consultation. He said: If after the term they wish to agree and to part, yes; and it is not marriage."
"13621","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that he heard Ibn Abbas consider it lawful even now, and informed me that he used to read: Whatever you enjoy of them to a term, so give them their wages. And Ibn Abbas said: In the reading, to a term. Ata said: And one I trust informed me, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: One of us would enjoy for a cupful of sawiq. And Safwan said: This is Ibn Abbas ruling adultery lawful. So Ibn Abbas said: I do not rule adultery lawful. Did Safwan forget Umm Arakah? By Allah, her son is from that; is that adultery? He said: And a man of Banu Jumah enjoyed her."
"13622","Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali, from Jabir ibn Abdillah and Salamah ibn al-Akwa, a man of Aslam of the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that they said: We were in a campaign, and the messenger of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, came and said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, says: Enjoy."
"13623","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Umar, Commander of the Believers, was only alerted by Umm Arakah, who came out pregnant. So Umar asked her about her pregnancy. She said: Salamah ibn Umayyah ibn Khalaf enjoyed me. When Safwan disapproved to Ibn Abbas some of what he says about that, he said: Ask your uncle whether he enjoyed."
"13624","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abdillah say: We, the Companions of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, enjoyed until Amr ibn Hurayth was forbidden. He said: And Jabir said: When the term ends, if they wish to return to each other, let him give her another dower. He said: And some of us asked him: How long does she wait? He said: One menstruation; they used to wait it for the one enjoyed."
"13625","And Abu al-Zubayr said: And I heard Jabir ibn Abdillah say: Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan enjoyed, upon his arrival from Taif to Thaqif, a freedwoman of Ibn al-Hadrami called Muanah. Jabir said: Then Muanah lived to Muawiyah's caliphate alive, and Muawiyah used to send her a gift every year until she died."
"13626","Abu al-Zubayr said: And I heard Tawus say: Ibn Safwan said: Ibn Abbas rules adultery lawful. He said: So Ibn Abbas listed men who were of the people of temporary marriage. He said: I remember of those he listed only Mabad ibn Umayyah."
"13627","Abu al-Zubayr said: I heard Jabir say: We used to enjoy for a handful of dates and flour in the days of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Abu Bakr, until people were forbidden in the affair of Amr ibn Hurayth."
"13628","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdillah say: Amr ibn Hurayth came from Kufah and enjoyed a freedwoman. She was brought to Umar pregnant, so he asked her. She said: Amr ibn Hurayth enjoyed me. So he asked him, and he told him of that as an open matter. He said: Why not another? That is when he forbade it."
"13629","Ibn Jurayj said: And one I trust informed me that Ali said in Kufah: Were it not for what preceded of the opinion of Umar ibn al-Khattab, or he said: of the opinion of Ibn al-Khattab, I would command temporary marriage, then none would commit adultery except a wretch."
"13630","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata: Can a man enjoy more than four together? Is enjoyment chastity? Is a woman's enjoyment lawful to her husband if he divorced her irrevocably? He said: I heard nothing about them, nor did I consult my companions about them."
"13631","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym informed me that Muhammad ibn al-Aswad ibn Khalaf informed him that Amr ibn Hawshab contracted temporary marriage with a virgin slave girl of Banu Amir ibn Luayy, and she became pregnant. That was mentioned to Umar, so he questioned her, and she said: Amr ibn Hawshab contracted muta with me. So he questioned him and he confessed. Umar said: Whom did you take as witnesses? He said: I do not know whether he said her mother, or her sister, or her brother and her mother. Then Umar stood upon the pulpit and said: What is the matter with men practicing muta without upright witnesses, and he did not explain it except that he had him flogged. He said: This statement about Umar was conveyed to me by one who was beneath his pulpit and heard him when he said it. He said: So the people took it up from him."
"13632","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that Hasan and Abdullah, the two sons of Muhammad, informed him, from their father Muhammad ibn Ali, that he heard his father Ali ibn Abi Talib saying to Ibn Abbas, when it reached him that he permitted muta: You are a confused man; the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade it on the day of Khaybar, as well as the meat of domestic donkeys."
"13633","From Mamar, he said: al-Zuhri informed me, from Khalid ibn al-Muhajir ibn Khalid, he said: Ibn Abbas permitted muta, so Ibn Abi Amrah al-Ansari said to him: What is this, O Abu Abbas? Ibn Abbas said: I did it with the Imam of the God-fearing. Ibn Abi Amrah said: O Allah, forgiveness; muta was only a concession like the necessity of carrion, blood and pork, then Allah perfected the religion afterwards."
"13634","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from al-Rabi ibn Sabrah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade temporary marriage with women."
"13635","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim: It was said to Ibn Umar: Ibn Abbas permits temporary marriage with women. He said: I do not think Ibn Abbas says this. They said: Yes, by Allah, he indeed says it. He said: By Allah, he would not have said this in the time of Umar; Umar would have punished you for the like of this, and I know it to be nothing but fornication."
"13636","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: I see its prohibition in the Quran. He said: So I said: Where? He said: Then he recited to me this verse: And those who guard their private parts, except from their wives or what their right hands possess."
"13637","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, he said: al-Qasim was asked about muta, so he recited this verse: Except from their wives or what their right hands possess."
"13638","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that Rabiah ibn Umayyah ibn Khalaf married a muwalladah of the muwalladat of Medina with the testimony of two women, one of them Khawlah bint Hakim, and she was a righteous woman. Then they were surprised only by the girl having become pregnant. Khawlah mentioned that to Umar ibn al-Khattab, so he stood up dragging the edge of his cloak out of anger until he ascended the pulpit and said: It has reached me that Rabiah ibn Umayyah married a muwalladah of Medina with the testimony of two women, and had I previously warned concerning this, I would have stoned."
"13639","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The scholars added a key for it when, the poet said: O companion, do you want the fatwa of Ibn Abbas."
"13640","From Mamar and al-Hasan, they said: Muta was never made lawful except three times in Umrat al-Qada; it was not made lawful before it nor after it."
"13641","From Mamar, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Umar, from Rabi ibn Sabrah, from his father, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, from Medina in the Farewell Hajj until, when we were at Usfan, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Umrah has entered into Hajj. Suraqah said to him: O Messenger of Allah, teach us the teaching of a people as though born today: is this umrah of ours for this year of ours or forever? He said: Rather forever. When we arrived at Mecca, we circumambulated the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, then he commanded us with temporary marriage with women. So we returned to him and said that they refused except for a named term. He said: Then do so. He said: So I went out, I and a companion of mine; upon me was a cloak and upon him was a cloak. We entered upon a woman and offered ourselves to her. She kept looking at my companion's cloak, seeing it better than my cloak, and looking at me, seeing me younger than him. She said: A cloak in place of a cloak, and she chose me. So I married her for my cloak and spent that night with her. In the morning I went to the mosque, and there was the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, upon the pulpit saying: Whoever had married a woman for a term, let him give her what he named for her, and let him not take back anything of what he gave her, and let him part from her; for Allah the Mighty and Majestic has forbidden it to you until the Day of Resurrection."
"13642","From al-Thawri, from Ismail ibn Abi Umayyah, from a man, he said: Ibn Umar was asked about muta, and he said: It is fornication."
"13643","From al-Thawri, from Malik ibn Mighwal, from al-Hasan, he said: Muta was only for three days, until Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His Messenger, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade it."
"13644","From al-Thawri, from a companion of his, from al-Hakam, he said: Ibn Masud said: Divorce, the waiting period and inheritance abrogated it."
"13645","From al-Thawri, from Dawud, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Inheritance abrogated it."
"13646","And I heard a man narrating to Mamar, he said: al-Ashath and al-Hajjaj ibn Artah informed me that they heard Abu Ishaq narrating, from al-Harith, from Ali, that he said: Ramadan abrogated every fast, zakat abrogated every charity, and divorce, the waiting period and inheritance abrogated muta. He said: And I heard other than al-Hajjaj narrating, from Muhammad, from Ali, he said: And sacrifice abrogated every slaughter."
"13647","From Israil ibn Yunus, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Ala, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: I heard Umar forbidding temporary marriage with women."
"13648","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail, from Qays, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: We used to campaign with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and our absence grew long, so we said: Shall we not castrate ourselves, O Messenger of Allah? He forbade us, then he permitted us to marry a woman for a term for something, then he forbade us from it on the day of Khaybar, and from the meat of domestic donkeys."
"13649","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was given the strength of forty, or forty-five, in intercourse; I doubt."
"13650","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Judan, he said: I heard Said ibn al-Musayyib saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was given the coital strength of forty-five men."
"13651","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was given the coitus of forty-five men, and he would not stay with any one of his wives a full day; he would come to this one at one hour and this one at one hour, moving among them thus through the day, until when it was night he apportioned to each woman her night."
"13652","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I was informed, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: I was given al-kafit. It was said: What is al-kafit? He said: The strength of thirty men in intercourse; and he had nine wives, and he would visit them all in one night. Ibn Jurayj said: Sulayman ibn Musa said: I asked whether the wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, were permitted to pray upon the roofs of the houses, and it was said to me: They would pray only upon the ground."
"13653","From Mamar and others: Wayhaka means waylaka, and al-wayl and waylaka are like wayhaka, like wayhaka."
"13654","Mamar informed us, from al-Hasan and Qatadah, concerning a man who dies owing a debt due at a term, they said: When he becomes insolvent or dies, his debt becomes due."
"13655","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, and from Ibn Tawus, from his father, they said: If they place the debt in a trustworthy hand, then it remains at its term."
"13656","Al-Thawri informed us, concerning a man who dies leaving a debt, then his heirs divide his property, then one of them becomes insolvent, he said: Begin with the one among them in whose possession property is found, and the heirs turn against one another. Ibn Jurayj said: From Ata and Amr ibn Dinar, the like of that."
"13657","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, arrived at Medina while they were advancing payment for fruits, so he said: Whoever advances payment for his fruit, it is usury, except by a known measure to a known term."
"13658","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Abu al-Minhal, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, arrived at Medina while they were advancing payment for fruits for two and three years, so the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Whoever advances payment for his fruit, then by a known measure to a known term. Al-Thawri informed us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Abu al-Minhal, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it, except that he said: Then the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: In a known measure and a known weight."
"13659","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub and Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he saw no harm in a man advancing silver for a thing to a known term and a known measure."
"13660","Mamar informed us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, he said: One who heard Ibn Umar informed me, saying: I wish a man had taken a dinar from me for food, bringing it to me from Syria."
"13661","And Mamar informed us, he said: A man asked Qatadah, saying: A man owes me food; he did not have it, so he bought it from the market. He said: I do not know; he brings it to me from wherever he wishes."
"13662","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, from Abu Hassan al-Araj, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I testify that guaranteed advance payment to a term Allah has made lawful and permitted; so why did Allah say: When you contract a debt to a named term."
"13663","Mamar informed us, from one who heard Ibrahim saying, concerning a man who advanced payment for fresh wheat of the year, then he delayed him to the next year, he said: He gives him from the fresh wheat of the year to which he delayed him."
"13664","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that he disliked advancing payment to the threshing floor, the juice, and the stipend, but one should name a month."
"13665","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah: He disliked advancing payment except to a known month."
"13666","Al-Thawri informed us, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Razin, from Ibn al-Musayyib: He was asked about advance payment for wheat, linens and garments, and he said: A known measure to a known term, and wheat by a known measure to a known term."
"13667","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Advance payment was permitted only because prices differ; you do not know whether it will be against you or not."
"13668","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that he disliked buying from a man while stipulating upon him at more or less than the price, saying: It is mine however the price turns out."
"13669","Al-Thawri said: When you advance a payment, specify it to a known term and in a known place; if you name the term and do not name the place, it is rejected until you name where he will deliver the food to you."
"13670","Al-Thawri informed us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Nubayh, from Abu Said, he said: Salam at whatever the price stands at is usury; rather you name with your dirhams a known measure, and seek increase with them as much as you can."
"13671","Ibrahim ibn Umar informed us, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, from Ibn Sirin, that he disliked advancing payment for food until it arrives."
"13672","Abd al-Karim said: And al-Hasan said: There is no harm in advance payment when it is a known measure to a known term."
"13673","He said: And Ibn Tawus used to say: Advance is made only to one who has crops or date palms."
"13674","I said to al-Thawri while I was in Mecca: I reside in this land and need fruit, and I advance a dirham for pomegranates, cucumbers, bananas and the like. He disliked it and said: Do not do it, for it varies."
"13675","Al-Thawri informed us, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from Muhammad ibn Abi al-Mujalid, he said: Ibn Abi Burdah and Abdullah ibn Shaddad sent me to Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza al-Khuzai and to Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa al-Aslami, so I asked them about advance payment, and they said: We would obtain booty in the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Nabateans of the Nabateans of Syria would come to us, so we would advance payment to them for wheat, barley and raisins to a known term. He said: I said: Did they have crops? They said: We would not ask them about that. Abd al-Razzaq said: And by this we take."
"13676","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, that he disliked the threshing, the stipend, the provision, the cutting and the harvest, but one should name a month. Al-jazaz means the cutting of date palms. From Mamar, from Qatadah, the like of it; and by it Abd al-Razzaq takes."
"13677","Mamar informed us, he said: Ali ibn Badhimah informed me that he heard Said ibn Jubayr, when asked about pledge and guarantor in advance payment, disliking it, saying: That is guaranteed profit."
"13678","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, that he disliked pledge and guarantor in advance payment."
"13679","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abdullah ibn Abi Yazid, from Abu Iyad, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, that he disliked pledge and guarantor in advance payment."
"13680","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Qays, he said: I heard Ibn Umar being asked about advance payment, known jars to a known term, and he saw no harm in it. It was said to him: He took a pledge. He said: That is the guaranteed deed."
"13681","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Hisham ibn Hujayr, he said: I heard al-Hasan al-Basri saying: The Muslims used to say: Whoever advances a payment, let him not take a pledge nor a heap watcher."
"13682","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: If advance payment is in principle without harm, then there is no harm in pledge and surety in it."
"13683","Mamar informed us, from Mansur and others, from Ibrahim and al-Shabi, that they saw no harm in advancing payment while taking a pledge or a surety."
"13684","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: There is no harm in pledge and guarantor in advance payment. From al-Thawri, from Mansur and al-Amash, from Ibrahim, the like of it."
"13685","Muhammad ibn Rashid informed us that he heard Makhul saying: There is no harm in pledge and guarantor in advance payment."
"13686","From al-Thawri, from Yazid, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, that he saw no harm in pledge and guarantor in advance payment."
"13687","Mamar informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, that a man was demanding the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, for a due, and he was harsh with him. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent to a Jew for advance payment from him, but he refused to advance him except with a pledge, so he sent to him his coat of mail, and said: By Allah, I am trustworthy on earth, trustworthy in heaven."
"13688","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, he said: I heard an old man of Bajilah saying: I heard al-Shabi saying, when asked about pledge and guarantor in advance payment, he said: It is more lawful than the water of the Euphrates."
"13689","Ismail ibn Abdullah informed us, he said: Ibn Awn narrated to me, he said: I asked al-Shabi about it, and he said: And who dislikes it? So I said: Shall I not narrate to you? He said: From the living or from the dead? I said: Rather from the living. He said: We have no need of your narration from the living."
"13690","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, bought from a Jew some sa of flour and pawned to him his coat of mail."
"13691","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, and from Mansur, from Ibrahim: They disliked that a man advance payment for a commodity, then take part of his commodity and part of his capital."
"13692","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif and Jabir, from al-Shabi, that he disliked part of his advance to be dirhams and part food."
"13693","From al-Thawri, from Mansur and al-Amash, from Ibrahim, that he disliked, when he advanced to a man for food, to take part of it as food and part as dirhams. He said: If he wants to do him a kindness, let him buy with the dirhams and leave for him what remains. Mamar informed us, from Qatadah and Mansur, from Ibrahim, the like of it. From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, the same equally. Mamar informed us, from Qatadah and al-Zuhri, the like of the saying of al-Hasan and Ibrahim."
"13694","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Ala, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, that he saw no harm, when a man advanced payment for food, in taking part of it as food and part as dirhams, saying: It is kindness."
"13695","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Salamah ibn Musa, he said: I asked Said ibn Jubayr about a man taking part of his capital and part of his advance, and he said: Ibn Abbas said: That is kindness."
"13696","Abdullah ibn Kathir informed us, from Shubah, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, that he said: There is no harm in it; it is kindness. He said: And al-Hakam saw no harm in it."
"13697","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Abu al-Sawda, he said: I and a brother of mine came forward to Shurayh, and I asked him about a man to whom we advanced salam; when the term fell due, he said: I do not have all the food; if you wish, take part of the food and part of your capital, and do kindness. He said: We said: We ask about that. He said: So we asked Shurayh, and he said: Either you take the food, or you take your capital. Al-Thawri informed us, from Jabir, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he saw no harm in it."
"13698","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, from Ibn Umar, he said: When you advance payment for something, take only your capital or that for which you advanced."
"13699","Al-Thawri informed us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: When you advance a payment, do not exchange it for anything until you take possession of it. Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman informed us, from Abd al-Karim, from al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin, the like of it."
"13700","From al-Thawri, from al-Hasan ibn Atiyyah al-Awfi, from his father, from Ibn Umar, he said: When you advance a payment, do not exchange it for anything until you take possession of it."
"13701","Hisham ibn Hassan informed us, from Muhammad, and al-Hasan, that they disliked, when you advanced payment by weight, to take by measure, or by measure to take by weight. And al-Thawri mentioned it, from Hisham, from al-Hasan and Muhammad, the like of it."
"13702","From al-Thawri, from Aslam, from Said ibn Jubayr, that he disliked that a man advance payment for kinds, saying: If it is wheat, you give me ten adhhab, and if it is barley, you give me twenty, and if it is dates, you give me thirty."
"13703","Mamar informed us, from Amr ibn Sulaym, he said: We asked Tawus, and I said: I advanced payment for something; may I exchange it for another? He said: There is no harm in exchanging it for another at value, except that you accept it and then take with the dinars whatever you wish. And by it Abu Bakr takes."
"13704","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Misar, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from Tawus, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about a man who advanced payment for fine sandals but did not find them with his companion; may he take sandals at their value? He disliked it and said: He takes from him nothing but that."
"13705","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Abu al-Shatha saying: When you advance payment for something, take only that for which you advanced, or your capital."
"13706","Ismail informed us, from Ibn Awn, from Ibn Sirin, that Ibn Umar disliked that phrase, to say: I advanced in such and such, saying: Islam is only for Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"13707","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: When you sell something for a dinar, then the term falls due, take with the dinar whatever you wish of that kind and of others."
"13708","From al-Thawri, from Hammad and Ibn Sirin, concerning a man who sold food for a dinar to a term, they said: He takes his food, or other, when it falls due."
"13709","Mamar informed us, he said: Tamim ibn Khuways informed me, from Abu al-Shatha, he said: When you sell for a dinar to a term, then the term falls due, take with the dinar whatever you wish, of that kind in which you advanced, or of others. And by it Abd al-Razzaq takes."
"13710","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, he said: I heard him narrating, from Ibn Abbas, that he was asked about a man who sold cloth; may he take wheat in its place? He said: There is no harm in it."
"13711","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When you advance payment for food, then the term falls due and you do not find food, take from him goods for less, and do not profit upon him twice."
"13712","He said: Jafar ibn Sulayman informed us, he said: A maternal cousin of mine informed me that he asked Mujahid, he said: I said: I sold to a man silk for a dinar to a term; when the term fell due, I found silk with him; may I take it from him? He said: Do not take it except for more than what you sold it to him for. Ibn Tawus said: Unless it had left his hand to another, then there is no harm in buying it for whatever you wish."
"13713","Mamar informed us, and others, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: When gold becomes due to you in a commodity, and he invites you to buy from him with it from other than the aspect of the commodity in which the gold was, then do so so long as you do not earn another profit; if you do, then do not grant him respite, and if you cancel it therein, then there is no harm."
"13714","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, he said: I said to Amr ibn Dinar: What do you think, if I sold food for gold, then the gold fell due, and I came seeking it, and he said: I do not have it; take food from me? He said: Tawus disliked taking food. And Abu al-Shatha said: When your debt falls due, take whatever you wish."
"13715","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri: When you sell something measured or weighed for a dinar, do not take anything measured or weighed unless he diverts you to other than that; and if you sell something measured and he diverts you to something weighed, take it, unless it is food."
"13716","He said: Malik informed us, from Abu al-Zinad, from Ibn al-Musayyib and Sulayman ibn Yasar, that they disliked, when you sold food for a dinar to a term, then the term fell due, taking food with it before taking possession of the gold."
"13717","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, he said: I witnessed Shurayh when two men came to him, one of whom had sold the other a camel, and he said: Cancel for me, and for you thirty dirhams. He said: Until I ask Shurayh. So he asked him, and I do not know what he answered him, except that I heard the man saying: I have accepted my camel, and accepted the thirty."
"13718","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, and from Ali ibn Badhimah, he said: I heard Said ibn Jubayr, when a man asked him about a man who bought a commodity from a man, then regretted it, and said: Cancel for me, and for you such and such. He said: There is no harm in it."
"13719","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, and from Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Tawus bought a slave boy, and he remained with him only a little until he returned him to his family, giving them ten dinars, and they would not accept him until he gave them the dinars."
"13720","Mamar informed us, he said: I asked Hammad about a man who bought a commodity from a man, then regretted it, and said: Cancel for me, and for you such and such. He disliked it."
"13721","Abdullah ibn Kathir informed us, from Shubah, he said: I asked al-Hakam ibn Utaybah and he disliked it. Al-Hakam said: And Mughirah informed me, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, that he sold a she-camel, and the one who bought it from him said to him: Take it, and for you forty dirhams. Al-Aswad did not take the dirhams and disliked it."
"13722","Mamar and al-Thawri informed us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, that he disliked returning it and returning something with it; this concerning one who buys a commodity and says: Cancel for me, and for you such and such."
"13723","Ibn Mujahid informed us, from his father, from Ibn Umar, that he saw no harm in it. Ibn Mujahid said: And Ata disliked it."
"13724","Mamar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade selling animal for animal on credit."
"13725","Al-Thawri and Israil informed us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Rafi, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah disliking animal for animal on credit."
"13726","Mamar informed us, from one who heard Ikrimah, when asked about a man who sold a camel for sheep to a term, he said: Those heads, nothing of them is sound for anything on credit."
"13727","Mamar said: And al-Hasan said: When they differ, there is no harm in it to a term; meaning sheep for cattle, cattle for camels, and the like of this."
"13728","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri: I asked him about animal for animal on credit, and he said: Ibn al-Musayyib was asked about it, and he said: There is no usury in animals. And he forbade madamin, malaqih and habal al-habalah. Al-madamin is what is in the loins of camels, al-malaqih is what is in their bellies, and habal al-habalah is the offspring of the offspring of this she-camel. Mamar informed us, and Ibn Uyaynah, from Ayyub, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, the like of it."
"13729","Malik informed us, from Abu al-Zinad, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that he said: There is no usury except in gold and silver, or in what is measured or weighed of what is eaten and drunk."
"13730","Mamar informed us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: He informed me that he asked Ibn Umar about a camel for two camels on credit, and he said: No, and disliked it. So my father asked Ibn Abbas, and he said: A camel may be better than two camels."
"13731","Mamar informed us, from Budayl al-Uqayli, from Mutarrif ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir, that Rafi ibn Khadij bought from him a camel for two camels; he gave him one of them and said: I will come to you tomorrow with the other, easily."
"13732","Al-Aslami informed me, and Malik, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali, he said: Ali sold a camel of his called Usayfir for twenty camels on credit."
"13733","Al-Aslami said: And Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr informed me, from Ibn Abi Qusayt, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Ali, that he disliked a camel for two camels on credit."
"13734","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Amr ibn Shuayb, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, commanded Abdullah ibn Amr to equip an army, and he said: We have no mounts. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to him: Buy for me mounts until the coming of the sadaqah camels. So Abdullah bought a camel for two camels and for more camels until the coming of the sadaqah camels."
"13735","Mamar informed us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent a collector, and he came to him with aged camels. When the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, saw him, he said: You have ruined yourself and ruined. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I was selling a young camel for two young camels, and three for an aged camel, hand to hand, knowing your need for mounts. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Then that is so, or: then no harm upon you."
"13736","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, and from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, they said: There is no harm in a camel for two camels, and a dirham, the dirham on credit. They said: If one of the two camels is on credit, then it is disliked."
"13737","Mamar informed us, from Hammad and others, from Ibrahim, he said: Abdullah ibn Masud was brought a man who advanced payment for young camels to a term, and he forbade him."
"13738","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that Abdullah disliked advance payment in animals."
"13739","Abdullah ibn Kathir informed us, from Shubah, he said: Qays ibn Muslim informed me, from Tariq ibn Shihab, he said: Zayd ibn Khulaydah advanced to Itris ibn Urqub for young she-camels, each young she-camel for fifty; when the term fell due, he came demanding him. He came to Ibn Masud seeking his intercession for respite, but Abdullah forbade him that and commanded him to take his capital. From al-Thawri, from Qays, from Tariq, the like of it."
"13740","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub and Qatadah, from al-Shabi, he said: Abdullah disliked it only because it was a stipulation from the offspring of the camel of so-and-so, and from the stallion of so-and-so."
"13741","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, that Umar disliked it. He said: And Shurayh used to dislike it."
"13742","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I heard Masruq saying: Shurayh advanced payment for two sound eloquent slaves of their tongue for a thousand dirhams; the man came with the two slaves, so Shurayh sold them for a thousand and four hundred, took the thousand and returned the four hundred to the owner of the two slaves."
"13743","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Umar, that he saw no harm in a man advancing payment in animals to a known term."
"13744","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: There is no harm in a man advancing payment in animals to a known term. Mamar informed us, from al-Hasan and al-Zuhri, the like of it."
"13745","From al-Thawri, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: A Bedouin came demanding a camel from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Seek for him a tooth like the tooth of his camel. They sought and found only above the age of his camel. The Bedouin said: You have paid me in full, may Allah pay you in full. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The best of you are the best of you in repayment."
"13746","Malik informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Rafi, freedman of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, borrowed a young camel from a man. Then camels of sadaqah came to him. Abu Rafi said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, commanded me to repay him a young camel. I said: I found only a choice four-year-old camel. He said: Repay it to him, for the best of people are the best of them in repayment. Mamar informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, with its chain, the like of it, except that he said: He commanded Bilal to repay him."
"13747","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Ammar al-Duhni, he said: I asked Said ibn Jubayr about salam in animals, and he said: Ibn Masud disliked it. I said: Will you not forbid these from it? He said: When you go spreading your merchandise upon one who does not want it, it breaks it."
"13748","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: You claim that we do not know the doors of usury; and that I know them is dearer to me than having the like of Egypt and its districts. Among matters are matters hidden from no one: that one buy gold with silver on credit, that one buy fruit while it is dyed red before it ripens, and that one advance payment for a tooth."
"13749","Mamar informed us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade selling meat for a living sheep. Zayd said, meaning: on credit or hand to hand."
"13750","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that he disliked selling a living thing for a dead one, meaning a standing sheep for a slaughtered one. Sufyan said: And we see no harm in it."
"13751","Mamar informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from a man, from Ibn Abbas, he said: There is no harm in selling meat for a sheep."
"13752","Al-Aslami informed us, from Salih, freedman of al-Tawamah, from Ibn Abbas, that a slaughter camel in the time of Abu Bakr was divided into ten parts, and a man said: Give me a part for a sheep. Abu Bakr said: This is not sound."
"13753","From al-Thawri, from Ashath ibn Abi al-Shatha, from Ubayd ibn Nadlah al-Khuzai, he said: A man slaughtered a camel, and a man took from it twenty for a young she-camel of the offspring of offspring. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, commanded him to return it."
"13754","Israil informed us, from Abdullah ibn Ismah, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas being asked about a man who bought a limb of a slaughter camel for the leg of a young she-goat, stipulating upon its owner that its mother nurse it until it is weaned. Ibn Abbas said: It is not sound."
"13755","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, that Zayd ibn Thabit and Ibn Umar saw no harm in selling allotments when they came out, but it is not lawful for one who bought them to sell them until he takes possession of them."
"13756","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, that he saw no harm in selling them when ordered therewith, but disliked for one who bought them to sell them until he takes possession of them."
"13757","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, that Hakim ibn Hizam used to buy provisions in the time of Umar from the jar, but Umar forbade him selling them until he takes possession of them."
"13758","Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, that he disliked saying: I sell you to a year; but if he says: Your stipend comes out to you before a year, my due becomes due."
"13759","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that he saw no harm in the worker saying to the owner of the provision: I give you two jaribs of barley for a jarib of wheat."
"13760","Al-Thawri said, from Yahya ibn Qays al-Kindi, from his grandmother, he said: I asked Shurayh about selling the increase in the stipend for offspring, and he disliked it, but saw no harm in it in animals."
"13761","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, that he disliked food, that anything of it be sold for anything on credit."
"13762","Mamar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: Whatever differs in kinds of food, there is no harm in it hand to hand: wheat for dates, raisins for barley; but he disliked it on credit."
"13763","From al-Thawri, from Musa ibn Abi Aishah, from Ibrahim, he said: Whatever is one thing measured, then like for like; when it differs, add and take increase, hand to hand."
"13764","Mamar informed us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, and from a man, from al-Hasan; and al-Thawri said: From Ibrahim, they said: Advance what is measured in what is weighed and not measured, and advance what is weighed and not measured in what is measured and not weighed."
"13765","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, that he saw no harm in wheat for flour, and flour for bread."
"13766","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, he said: A mudd of flour for a mudd of wheat is not sound except by weight. I heard al-Thawri giving fatwa by the saying of Qatadah, and by it he takes."
"13767","And we asked Mamar about flour, a mudd for two mudds, and he said: al-Hasan and Qatadah saw no harm in it when it differed."
"13768","Abdullah informed us, from Shubah, he said: I asked al-Hakam and Hammad about a mudd of wheat for a mudd of flour, and they disliked it."
"13769","Mamar informed us, from Qatadah, he said: There is no harm in flour for bread, and wheat for bread, hand to hand. He said: It has left measuring."
"13770","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, that he disliked sawiq for wheat like for like, because there is excess in it. Sufyan said: Wheat for flour on credit is disliked, but he sees no harm in bread for flour on credit."
"13771","Malik informed us, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, freedman of al-Aswad ibn Sufyan, that Zayd Abu Ayyash, freedman of Abu Zuhrah, informed him that he asked Sad ibn Abi Waqqas about white wheat for sult, and Sad said to him: Which of them is better? He said: The white. He said: Then he forbade me from it, and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, being asked about buying dates for fresh dates, and he said: Does the fresh date decrease when it dries? They said: Yes. So he forbade it."
"13772","From al-Thawri, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Zayd Abu Ayyash, freedman of Banu Zuhrah, from Sad, he said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was asked about fresh dates for dry dates, and he said to those around him: Does it decrease when it dries? It was said: Yes. So he forbade it. He said: And Sad was asked about sult for white wheat, and he narrated this."
"13773","Al-Thawri informed us, from Tariq, from Ibn al-Musayyib: A qafiz of fresh dates for a qafiz of dry was disliked."
"13774","Mamar informed us, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, he said: The family of Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad gave a sa of wheat for two sa of barley as fodder for his horse, so he commanded them to return it."
"13775","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim, and a man, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that dates belonging to Bilal changed, so Bilal went out with them to the market and sold them two sa for a sa. When that reached the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, he disapproved it and said: What is this, O Bilal? He informed him, and he said: You have dealt in usury; return our dates to us."
"13776","Malik reported from Nafi', from Sulayman ibn Yasar, that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad ibn 'Abd Yaghuth ran out of fodder for his riding animal, so he said to his servant: Take from your family's wheat and buy barley with it, but take only the same measure."
"13777","Ma'mar reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama, from Abu Sa'id, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon one of his family members and found with them dates better than their own dates, so he said: Where is this from? They said: We exchanged two sa' for one sa'. He said: No two sa' for one sa', and no two dirhams for one dirham."
"13778","Al-Thawri said about one date for two dates: It is disliked, because its basis is measure."
"13779","Al-Thawri said: from Abu [lacuna in source], from Abu Qilaba, from Abu al-Ash'ath, from 'Ubada ibn al-Samit, who said: Mu'awiya used to sell silver vessels for more than their weight, so 'Ubada said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Gold for gold, weight for weight; silver for silver, weight for weight; wheat for wheat, like for like; barley for barley, like for like; dates for dates, like for like; salt for salt, like for like; and sell gold for silver hand to hand as you wish, and wheat for barley hand to hand as you wish. Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, something like this, and that reached Mu'awiya, so he said: What is with people narrating reports, when we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and did not hear them? 'Ubada said: We narrate what we heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), even if the nose of Mu'awiya is rubbed in dust. [source has a lacuna in one narrator's name]"
"13780","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to dislike meat for wheat on credit."
"13781","We asked al-Thawri, and he said: This is the best of sales in our view."
"13782","Ma'mar reported from Hammad, from Ibrahim, and al-Thawri reported from Mughira, from Ibrahim: He saw no harm in one garment for two garments on credit when the two differ, and he disliked it when of one kind. Al-Thawri said, from Mughira: No harm in one person for two persons when the two differ."
"13783","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi: He saw no harm in it."
"13784","Ma'mar and al-Thawri reported from Isma'il ibn Umayya, from Ibn al-Musayyib, about one Qibti garment for two Qibti garments on credit: He saw no harm in it, and said: Riba is only in what is measured or weighed."
"13785","Ma'mar reported from a man, from al-Hakam ibn 'Utayba, who said: Two garments for one garment with a delay is not forbidden; that was when he was asked about one taq shawl for two kirbas cloths. Ibn Jurayj said it from 'Ata'."
"13786","'Amr reported from one who heard al-Hasan say: When two kinds of trade goods that are neither measured nor weighed differ, there is no harm in selling one taq for two kirbas cloths, advancing one of the two exchanges."
"13787","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: It defeats me to know what counted as trade goods when some of them are sold for others on credit."
"13788","I asked Ma'mar about a garment for yarn on credit, both of 'utb cotton, and he said: Al-Hasan used to dislike it, but saw no harm in yarn of 'utb for a garment of karabis linen on credit."
"13789","Ibn al-Taymi reported from his father, who said: I asked Tawus about advance payment for trade goods, and he said: No harm in it. And I asked him about advance payment for silk, and he said: I do not know what silk is."
"13790","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib and Jabir ibn Zayd, who both said: No harm in iron for copper on credit. He said: And al-Hasan used to dislike it."
"13791","From al-Thawri about iron for copper, he said: No harm in it hand to hand, and it is disliked on credit."
"13792","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: Everything that is weighed follows the course of gold and silver, and everything that is measured follows the course of wheat and barley."
"13793","'Abd Allah ibn Kathir reported from Shu'ba, who said: I asked al-Hakam and Hammad about iron for copper on credit, and he said: No harm in it; and Hammad disliked it."
"13794","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, who said: No harm in one fals for two fulus."
"13795","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever buys food, let him not sell it until he takes possession of it. Ibn 'Abbas said: And I deem everything to be in the position of food. From 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, except that he said: Until he takes full possession of it."
"13796","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, from a man, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Hakim ibn Hizam: Do not sell what is not with you. 'Abd al-Razzaq said: And Ibn Sirin used to narrate it from Ayyub."
"13797","Ma'mar reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir that 'Uthman ibn 'Affan and Hakim ibn Hizam used to buy dates and put them in sacks, then sell them by that same measure, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade them to sell them until they measure them out for those who bought them from them."
"13798","'Umar ibn Rashid, or another, reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Isma, from Hakim ibn Hizam, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I make purchases; what of them is lawful for me and what is forbidden to me? He said: O son of my brother, when you buy a purchase, do not sell it until you take possession of it."
"13799","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a loan combined with a sale, two conditions in one sale, selling what is not with you, and profit on what you have not guaranteed."
"13800","Ibn 'Uyayna reported from 'Amr ibn Dinar, and from Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, who said: I heard Nafi' ibn Jubayr say: I sold food to 'Amr ibn 'Uthman, the food prompt and the payment deferred, part of it with me and part of it not with me, so I sent to Ibn 'Abbas and Ibn 'Umar, and a messenger came to me from them both: As for what was with you, then delay it, and what was not with you, then return it."
"13801","Ma'mar reported, he said: I said to Qatada: I bought food while a man was watching me as I measured it; may I sell it to him by that measuring? He said: No, until he measures it out from you."
"13802","Al-Thawri reported from Mutarrif, from al-Sha'bi, who said: At every sale is its own measuring. And 'Abd al-Razzaq holds this view. Al-Thawri said about two men trading food, both measuring it, then one of them giving the other a profit in it, he said: No, until he measures it with another measuring; each one of them measures his share, then he measures his share for the one to whom he gave the profit."
"13803","Ma'mar reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salama ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, about a man who buys dates on the tops of palm trees: He should not sell them until he harvests them. He said: And Sulayman ibn Yasar said: No harm in it."
"13804","Ja'far ibn Sulayman reported from Hisham ibn Hassan, from al-Zubayr ibn Khirrit, from 'Ikrima, from Ibn 'Abbas: He disliked, when a man buys dates on the tops of palm trees, that he sell them until he harvests them."
"13805","Ibn al-Taymi reported from a man who heard Qatada narrate from Sulayman ibn Yasar that Zayd ibn Thabit and al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam said: When a man buys dates on the tops of palm trees, there is no harm in his selling them before he harvests them."
"13806","Ibn Jurayj reported from 'Ata' al-Khurasani that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As said: O Messenger of Allah, we hear reports from you; do you permit me to write them down? He said: Yes. He said: The first thing the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to the people of Mecca was a letter: Two conditions in one sale are not allowed; a sale and a loan together; selling what one has not guaranteed; and whoever was a mukatab for one hundred dirhams and pays all of them but one dirham is still a slave, or for one hundred uqiyyas and pays all of them [source has a gap here] but one uqiyya is still a slave. [source has a gap in the second clause]"
"13807","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: Muwasafa is muwata'a. By this he said: He used to dislike muwasafa. Muwasafa is that a man describes goods that are not with him. And he also disliked that you come to a man with a garment that is not yours and say: Is this what you need? Then when he says yes, you buy it in order to sell it to him on credit."
"13808","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, that he asked him about a man who says: Buy such-and-such cloth and I will buy it from you; and he disliked it."
"13809","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, or another, from al-Hasan: He used to dislike that a man comes to you bargaining over something that is not with you, and you say: Come back to me tomorrow, while you intend to buy it for him."
"13810","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: Do not bargain with him and do not promise him; say: It is not with me."
"13811","Ma'mar reported, he said: I heard Ja'far ibn Burqan ask al-Zuhri, he said: A man comes to me seeking goods with me, but they are not with me, so I send to a man who has them, and he sends them to me, and I show them to the man and say: Is this what you need? He says yes, so I buy it from its owner and sell it to him; and he disliked it. Ja'far said: We deemed it among the best of sales. Al-Zuhri said: It is disliked."
"13812","Ma'mar reported that al-Hasan and Qatada used to dislike all muwasafa, in his view, in food and in other things."
"13813","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Zayd ibn Aslam told me, he said: I was with Ibn 'Umar when a broker asked him, saying: A man comes to me about a camel that is not mine; he bargains with me and I sell it to him, then I buy it for cash. Ibn 'Umar said: No."
"13814","Ibn Jurayj said: And Abu al-Zubayr told me that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah dislike it and say: Do not sell a sale until you take possession of it."
"13815","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: No harm in buying something that is neither measured nor weighed for cash, then selling it before taking possession of it. Ma'mar reported from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, the same."
"13816","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, from al-Hasan: He disliked it."
"13817","Ibn 'Uyayna reported from Ibn Shubruma, who said: No harm in it."
"13818","Malik and Ibn 'Uyayna reported from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, who said: I was with Ibn 'Abbas when a man came who had paid in advance for saba'ib cloths; may he sell them before taking possession of them? Ibn 'Abbas said: No; that is but silver for silver and gold for gold."
"13819","Ibn Jurayj reported from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, who said: Do not sell a sale until you take possession of it."
"13820","Ibn 'Uyayna reported from Yahya ibn Sa'id, who said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyib say: When you buy something that is measured or weighed, do not sell it until you take possession of it."
"13821","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: When a man buys from you something upon description and you do not depart from what you described to him, the sale is binding upon him. Ayyub said: And al-Hasan said: He has the option when he sees it."
"13822","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: I saw Shurayh when two men came to him; one of them said: This man sold me a garment like this one for so-and-so, then he brought it to me, but I had bought from him its like, I did not buy this very one from him. He said: Do you find anything more like it than it? And he upheld it against him."
"13823","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Sheep passed by a man, so he said: Whose are these? They said: So-and-so's; he bought them from so-and-so. He went to him and said: Sell me your sheep that you bought from so-and-so. He said: Yes, and sold them to him. Then he disputed with him before Shurayh, saying: I saw fat, huge sheep. The other said: I do not know what this man says, but he bought from me my sheep that I bought from so-and-so. Shurayh said: For you are the sheep of so-and-so that were bought from so-and-so."
"13824","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: The Companions of the Prophet said: We wish 'Uthman ibn 'Affan and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf would trade with each other, so we could see which of them has greater fortune in trade. He said: 'Abd al-Rahman bought from 'Uthman a horse from another land for forty thousand dirhams, or four thousand, or something like that, if the deal reached it while it was sound. Then he went a little and came back and said: I will add you six thousand if my messenger finds it sound? He said: Yes. The messenger of 'Abd al-Rahman found it had perished, and he came out of it by the other condition. A man said to al-Zuhri: What if he did not stipulate? He said: It is from the property of the seller."
"13825","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: There is no harm in a man buying an absent animal when he knew it; if it is sound today, then it is upon me."
"13826","Al-Thawri reported, he said: Every deal made upon description, if there is nothing like it, then its owner has the option when he sees it."
"13827","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: Whoever buys something decisively and the buyer wants to take possession of it, but the seller says: I will not give it to you until you pay me, and then it perishes, it is from the property of the seller, because he held it back as a pledge. But if he says: Take your goods, and he says: Leave it until I send to you one who will take possession of it, and then it perishes, it is from the property of the buyer. Ma'mar said: But if both are silent, then Hammad, Ibn Shubruma and others deem it nothing until he takes possession of it. Ma'mar reported from Qatada the same as the word of Tawus, from al-Thawri."
"13828","From al-Thawri, from Ibn 'Awn, from Ibrahim, about a man who sells a man goods and says: Take it, but the buyer says: Leave it with you, and then it dies: When he offered it to him and he did not accept it, it is from the property of the buyer. Sufyan said: As for our companions, they say: No, until he takes possession of it."
"13829","Ibn al-Taymi reported from Ash'ath, from al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin, who both said: Liability is upon the seller until the buyer takes possession of it."
"13830","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Shubruma, who said: Whoever buys a slave girl and places her in the hands of a man to establish freedom from pregnancy, and she dies before menstruating, she is from the property of the seller."
"13831","Al-Thawri said: About a slave girl whom the two traders place for establishing freedom from pregnancy and she perishes, he said: If the buyer has not taken possession of her, she is from the property of the seller. From our companions."
"13832","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, who said: She is from the property of the buyer, so long as her pregnancy has not become manifest."
"13833","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: It is upon the one on whom liability was stipulated, the seller or the buyer."
"13834","Al-Thawri said: About a man who sold a garment, and the buyer did not take possession of it until another came after him; one valued the garment at ten dirhams and the other valued the garment at five; he said: Its price belongs to the seller, because the buyer had not guaranteed it, so he has no profit on what he has not guaranteed. [wording of the valuations obscure in source]"
"13835","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, who said: Tawliya is a sale, in food and in other things."
"13836","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from al-Hasan, who said: No harm in tawliya; it is but a favor."
"13837","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir and Zakariyya, from al-Sha'bi, and from Sulayman al-Taymi, from al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin, and from Fitr, from al-Hakam, who said: Tawliya is a sale. Al-Thawri said: And we say: Partnership is a sale, and none enters partnership until he takes possession."
"13838","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: No harm in tawliya; it is but a favor. He said: And Ibn Sirin said: No, until he takes possession and it is measured."
"13839","Ibn al-Taymi reported from his father, from al-Hasan and Muhammad: They both disliked tawliya unless it is measured."
"13840","Ma'mar reported from Rabi'a, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that the Prophet said: Tawliya, iqala and partnership are alike; no harm in them."
"13841","As for Ibn Jurayj, he said: Rabi'a ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman told me, from the Prophet, a report widespread in Medina, he said: Whoever buys food, let him not sell it until he takes possession of it and takes full measure of it, unless he shares it with another, or passes it on at cost, or cancels the sale."
"13842","Ma'mar reported from Qatada about two partners who bought goods, then one of them bought out the other with an excess, he said: No harm in that in what is neither measured nor weighed."
"13843","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: No harm for two partners sharing goods or merchandise that is neither measured nor weighed, that one of them buys out the other's share before they divide."
"13844","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, who said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyib about a man who owns a share in sheep; may he sell it before it is divided? He said: Yes. I said: The Prophet forbade selling spoils before they are divided. He said: Spoils contain gold and silver. Ma'mar said: And he does not know how much his share of the spoils is."
"13845","Ma'mar and Ibn 'Uyayna reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bought a camel, or something else, from a bedouin before prophethood, and after the sale the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Choose. The bedouin looked at him and said: May Allah grant you long life; who are you? Then when Islam came, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) instituted the option after the sale."
"13846","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two traders have the option so long as they have not parted, unless it be a sale with an option. 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar reported from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"13847","'Abd Allah ibn Muharrar reported, he said: Thabit ibn al-Hajjaj told me, from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfa, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Sale is by mutual consent, and giving the option is by the striking of the deal."
"13848","From al-Thawri, from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In every sale between two, there is no sale between them until they part, except a sale with an option."
"13849","Ma'mar reported from Isma'il ibn Umayya, from Nafi', who said: Ibn 'Umar, when he bought something, would walk a little while to conclude the sale, then return."
"13850","Al-Thawri reported from Abu 'Attab, from Abu Zar'a, that a man bargained with him over a horse of his; when he sold it to him he gave him the option three times, then said: Choose. Each one of them gave his companion the option three times. Then Abu Zar'a said: I heard Abu Hurayra say: Thus is sale by mutual consent."
"13851","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to the people of al-Baqi' and called out in his voice: O people of al-Baqi', two traders do not part except by consent."
"13852","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, who said: I saw him when a dispute was brought to him about a man who bought a purchase from a man and said: I was not pleased with it, while the other said: You were indeed pleased with it. He said: Your proof that you both parted by consent after the sale, or by option, or his oath by Allah that you did not part by mutual consent after the sale and not by option."
"13853","Ibn 'Uyayna reported from Sulayman al-Ahwal, who said: I heard Tawus swear by Allah: Giving the option is only after the sale."
"13854","From al-Thawri, from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Safar, from al-Sha'bi, from Shurayh, who said: The two traders have the option so long as they have not parted."
"13855","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, who said: Ibrahim deemed the sale binding by speech when the two trade, even if they have not parted."
"13856","From al-Thawri, from al-Hajjaj, raising it to 'Umar, that 'Umar said at Mina when he put his foot in the stirrup: People will say tomorrow: What did 'Umar say? Lo, sale is only by the striking of the deal or by option, and the Muslim is bound by his condition. Sufyan said: And the striking of the deal is by the tongue."
"13857","Hushaym reported from al-Hajjaj, from Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn al-Zubayr, from a man of Kinana, who said: 'Umar said when he put his foot in the stirrup at Mina: Hear what I say to you, and do not say: 'Umar said, 'Umar said. Sale is by the striking of the deal or by option, and every Muslim is bound by his condition."
"13858","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, about a man who buys goods on approval, he said: The option belongs to both of them until they part by consent."
"13859","From al-Thawri, from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar, who said: I heard Ibn 'Umar say: I used to buy if I was pleased, until 'Abd Allah ibn Muti' bought a Bactrian she-camel if he was pleased with it; he said: A man is pleased and then leaves it, and it was as if it woke me up; so he would buy and then say: Here, if you take. [sense obscure in source]"
"13860","Al-Thawri said about a man who sold a garment, saying: I have taken it for so-and-so; does he stipulate approval? He said: If no term is fixed for approval, the sale is void; whichever of them wishes may return it."
"13861","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: When you sell something on approval and he pays you the silver, do not mix it with other money until you see whether he takes or returns."
"13862","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: A man bought a purchase from a man and said: If you bring me the payment by such-and-such a day, otherwise there is no sale between me and you; then he came to him the next day, and they disputed before Shurayh, so Shurayh said: You broke your promise with him. Ibn Jurayj reported, and 'Ata' said: This is no sale."
"13863","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, about a man who bought goods on approval and named the price, then they perished, he said: He is liable."
"13864","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, who said: He swears by Allah that he was not pleased; if he swears, there is no liability upon him."
"13865","From al-Thawri, who said: When he takes it upon bargaining without naming the price and it perishes, there is no liability upon him."
"13866","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir, from 'Amir, from Salman ibn Rabi'a, who was asked about a man who bought goods from a man on condition of looking at them, with the price fixed, then they died: He is liable."
"13867","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from his father, and from 'Amr ibn Muslim, from Tawus, about a man who took a garment from a man, saying: Go with it; if you are pleased with it, take it; then he sold it before returning to the man, he said: It is binding upon him when he sells it. 'Amr said: So I asked 'Ikrima, and he said: No profit is lawful for him. Ma'mar said: And the word of Tawus is dearer to me."
"13868","Bakkar reported, he said: I heard Wahb ibn Munabbih asked about it, and he said: It is binding upon him when he sells it."
"13869","Al-Thawri reported: About a man who sold something on approval, and the buyer named a term within which to return it; if he keeps it beyond the condition fixed for him, the sale is binding upon him. If the buyer dies within the condition before it is known whether he was pleased or not, his heirs are bound. If the seller dies while the buyer is within his term, it remains upon his condition; he returns it to the heirs of the seller if he wishes."
"13870","Al-Thawri reported: About a man who took two garments from a man on condition of approving one of them, and both perished after the price was named, he said: He pays half of their two prices; if one of them perishes, he guarantees it."
"13871","Ma'mar and al-Thawri reported from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: Every sale in which there is a condition, the condition is void, except manumission; and every marriage in which there is a condition, the condition is void, except divorce."
"13872","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Abi Najih, who said: Whoever stipulates a condition and reduces the price for it, the condition is void, and what he reduced is returned to him."
"13873","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Utba, who said: Ibn Mas'ud wanted to buy from his wife a slave girl to take as a concubine, so she said: No, I will not sell her to you unless you stipulate to me that if you sell her, you sell her to me, for I have the first right to her at the price. He said: Until I ask 'Umar. He asked him, and he said: Do not approach her while anyone has a condition in her."
"13874","Al-Thawri reported from 'Asim ibn 'Ubayd Allah, from al-Qasim, that 'A'isha disliked that a slave girl be sold with a condition."
"13875","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, who said: I sold a slave girl of my father's, stipulating that she not be sold nor given away. I said to Ibn Tawus: 'Umar said: Do not approach her while anyone has a condition in her. He said: There is no condition in her; it is only for her own sake."
"13876","Ma'mar reported from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from 'Ikrima, about a man who buys a purchase, and the seller says to the buyer: No liability upon you if it depreciates, he said: This is no sale."
"13877","From al-Thawri, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Rafi', who said: I disputed before Shurayh about a slave girl I sold to a man, when news of his bankruptcy reached me, so I said: Take for me from him a guarantor. He said: Your property is where you put it. I said: I had stipulated that if [trouble] reaches me, then here I am. He said: You have acknowledged the sale, so your proof is upon the condition. [wording of the condition obscure in source]"
"13878","Isma'il ibn 'Abd Allah reported, he said: 'Ubayd Allah ibn al-'Izar told me, he said: I disputed before Shurayh about [lacuna in source] of mine against a man, so I said: Take for me from him a guarantor until I bring witnesses. He said: Your witnesses came and nothing was established against him after that. [source has a lacuna]"
"13879","Abu Sufyan reported from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, who said: A woman came to al-Sha'bi and said: My daughter was sold on condition that she not be sold. He said: Your daughter remains upon her condition."
"13880","He said: And al-Thawri told me, from Shabib ibn Gharqada, who said: I heard Shurayh say: Every Muslim is bound by his condition."
"13881","Ibn Jurayj reported from 'Ata' about a man who sells a man a slave girl on condition that you take her as a concubine, not sell her, not practice withdrawal with her, and on condition that if you bring the payment by such-and-such a day, otherwise there is no sale between me and you, he said: This is no sale; she belongs to the seller. And every sale in which there is a condition is no sale. He said: And 'Amr ibn Dinar said: No harm in that."
"13882","Ma'mar reported from Qatada and Hammad about a man who says to a man: I hire from you to Mecca for so-and-so, and if I travel a month, or so-and-so, then for you is an extra so-and-so; they saw no harm in it. And it was disliked that he say: I hire from you for so-and-so on condition that you travel a month, and if I travel less than a month, you deduct so-and-so from it."
"13883","Ma'mar reported, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about a man who hires from a man his food transport to one of these mines, saying: I hire from you for so-and-so on condition that you travel a month, and if you travel more than a month, my food is upon you as a sale, every sa' for a dirham. He said: This condition is not allowed."
"13884","Al-Thawri reported: About a man who hires transport of food to a mine, each camel for two dinars, on condition that you meet me on such-and-such a day, and if you do not meet me on such-and-such a day, my food is upon you as a sale for so-and-so, he said: This is not sound."
"13885","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: A dispute was brought to Shurayh about a man who hired from a man his mount, saying: If I do not leave on such-and-such a day, for you is an extra so-and-so; then he did not leave on that day and detained him. Shurayh said: Whoever stipulates upon himself a condition willingly, uncoerced, we enforce it against him."
"13886","Ibn Jurayj reported from 'Ata', who said: I said to him: A man hires transport to Mecca, going and returning, for so-and-so, and if I stay behind, then I get so much of the hire. He said: No."
"13887","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, from al-Muttalib ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Hantab, that the Prophet bought a camel from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, and lent him its back to ride to Medina."
"13888","Al-Thawri reported from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, that 'Umar used to dislike that a reduction be sought after the sale became binding."
"13889","Ma'mar reported from Harun ibn Ri'ab, who said: Ibn 'Umar bought a camel and passed by people with it, telling them for how much he took it. They said to him: Go back and ask its owner for a reduction, for he will reduce for you. He said: No, I am satisfied with it."
"13890","From Thawr, from Jabir, who said: Whoever saw Ibn 'Umar say to his servant: When you buy meat for a dirham, do not ask for anything extra."
"13891","Ibn 'Uyayna reported from Tawus, from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq, from a man, who said: 'Ali passed by a slave girl buying meat from a butcher while saying: Give me extra. 'Ali said: Give her extra, for it is more blessed for the sale."
"13892","From al-Thawri, from Abu Sinan, from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl; al-Thawri said: And Ajlah narrated it to me from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, who said: I saw 'Ammar ibn Yasir buy cucumbers for dirhams, and I saw him dispute with its owner over a rope after the sale became binding; I do not know which of them overcame the other. Then he took it and carried it on his back until he brought it to the palace."
"13893","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, who said: I heard him say about a man who remits part of his right and then retracts it; I heard him say to the one for whom the right was remitted: Your proof that he remitted it while able to take it. And he does not allow coercion or pressure."
"13894","Ma'mar reported from Ibn Tawus, who said: 'Urwa ibn Muhammad wrote to a man at al-Jund to judge between two men, and wrote to him not to judge between them until he asks Tawus. He asked him, and I do not know what was between them, except that I heard my father say: Know that no coerced sale is allowed."
"13895","From Isra'il, from Abu al-Haytham, who said: I said to Ibrahim: A man is being tormented; shall I buy from him? He said: No."
"13896","Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling a date palm for dates, and selling fruit before its soundness appears."
"13897","Malik reported from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling fruit before its soundness appears, binding both the seller and the buyer."
"13898","Ma'mar and Ibn 'Uyayna reported from al-Zuhri, from Kharija ibn Zayd, from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said, while in Medina: Do not buy fruit until the Pleiades rises. Al-Zuhri said: So I mentioned that to Salim ibn 'Abd Allah, and he said: Blight occurs even after that."
"13899","Ma'mar reported from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Selling fruit before its soundness appears was forbidden, and ears of grain before they whiten, and unripe dates before they ripen. He said: And some of them say: Until the grain is rubbed."
"13900","Ibn 'Uyayna reported from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: I do not know whether he raised it to the Prophet; he said: He forbade selling fruit before it becomes edible. He said: And Ibn 'Umar said: Until its soundness appears."
"13901","Ibn 'Uyayna reported from 'Amr, from al-Hasan, who said: The Messenger of Allah forbade selling wheat before it hardens in its husks."
"13902","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Najrani, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: A man bought palm trees from a man, and that year produced nothing, so they disputed before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How do you deem his dirhams lawful? Return his dirhams to him, and do not pay in advance for palm trees until their soundness appears. He said: So I asked Masruq what its soundness is, and he said: They redden and yellow."
"13903","From al-Thawri, from a shaykh of theirs, from Anas, who said: The Prophet forbade selling palm trees before they ripen, grain before it is rubbed hard, and fruits before they become edible."
"13904","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-'Awfi, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Prophet said: Do not buy fruit before its soundness appears. He said: And when does its soundness appear? He said: Until its blight goes and its wholesome part is pure."
"13905","Hisham ibn Hassan reported from Anas ibn Sirin, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: When part of the palm trees redden, it suffices him to sell them. From al-Thawri and Hisham and others, from Anas ibn Sirin, from Ibn 'Abbas, the same."
"13906","Sufyan said about one who buys fruit and then it bears more fruit, he said: For him is what came out the first time."
"13907","Ma'mar reported from Jabir, from 'Amir, that 'Umar and Ibn Mas'ud said: The fruit of palm trees is not sold until it reddens and yellows."
"13908","Muhammad ibn Rashid reported from Yazid ibn Ya'fur that he heard al-Hasan say: The Messenger of Allah forbade selling unripe dates before they yellow, grapes before they blacken, and grain before it hardens in its husks."
"13909","Ma'mar reported from Qatada, who said: When fruit becomes edible, its sale is lawful. He said: And when its edible part is more than the other, its sale is lawful."
"13910","Ibn 'Uyayna reported from al-Hasan ibn 'Umara, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, who said about peaches, apples, pears and their likes: They are sold when they set; saying: When they become grain. [sense obscure in source]"
"13911","Ibn 'Uyayna reported from 'Amr ibn Dinar, who said: I heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah say: I forbade Ibn al-Zubayr from selling palm trees years in advance."
"13912","Ibn 'Uyayna reported from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Abu Ja'far, who said: The Prophet wrote a charity order to me, so I went to Mahmud ibn Labid and asked him. He said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab used to sell an orphan's property under his care for three years, meaning its fruit. [wording obscure in source]"
"13913","Ibn 'Uyayna reported from Hisham ibn 'Urwa, from his father, that 'Umar used to sell an orphan's property under his care for three years, meaning its fruit."
"13914","Ma'mar reported from one who heard 'Ikrima say: Beware of sirar sales, for sirar sales are not sound; they return to liability and regret."
"13915","Al-Thawri reported from Mughira, from Ibrahim, who said: He used to forbid the throwing of the stone."
